In light of these considerations, we develop CYTransformer---an encoder-decoder transformer model \cite{Vaswani:2017lxt} to automate the generation of toric Calabi-Yau spaces. Transformers are machine learning models designed to process and generate sequential data in an autoregressive manner. Their self-attention mechanisms allows them to capture complex dependencies within the data, and they have proved in recent years to be widely successful at sequence modeling tasks, and in particular at natural language processing. The transformer architecture underpins all state-of-the-art large language models \cite{Touvron2023LLaMAOA,Jiang2023Mistral7,Achiam2023GPT4TR,Llama3} and has made the recent generative AI revolution possible. While transformers' ability to model natural language and code is now well-established, they have also been applied to more exotic data distributions coming from mathematics \cite{Funsearch, charton2024patternboost, Alfarano}, physics \cite{Geneva2020TransformersFM, Janny2023EagleLL,  Cai:2024znx, Smatrix} or chemistry \cite{jumper2021highly}. Unlike natural languages and programming languages, to whose shared grammatical and logical structures \cite{Inductionheads, Iterationhead, FunctionVI} transformers are known to be well-suited, data distributions directly arising from new scientific problems differ wildly, which gives rise to unique challenges. In particular, finding an efficient (and transformer-compatible) representation for a given problem is often a non-trivial task.

In our approach, the coordinates of the vertices of a given polytope are used as inputs to the transformer's encoder, and the transformer's decoder outputs a sequence of symbols, called \textit{tokens}, which represents a candidate triangulation of the polytope. Within the sequence, each token corresponds to a simplex. With this setup, the probabilistic generation of the next simplex in the sequence is conditioned on all previous ones, as well as on the selected polytope. Our model successfully learns the complicated structure of FRSTs, which allows it to generate new Calabi-Yau manifolds. Namely, we show that our transformer model trained on a given set of polytopes and FRSTs learns to generate new FRSTs not only for the polytopes within its training set, but also for yet unseen polytopes. Moreover, we observe that the FRSTs generated by our transformer model are representative of the entire ensemble. Finally, we show that our model can keep improving beyond the limitations of its training data by repeatedly generating new FRSTs and training on them. This method, which we simply refer to as \textit{self-improvement} in what follows, can be seen as a form of reinforcement learning with rejection sampling, which has recently become a staple of the training of large language models \cite{R1}. It offers a way to mitigate the effects of the relative difficulty of obtaining diverse training data for large polytopes.

% \ca{End of suggested modification---}

These promising results, especially those pertaining to self-improvement, motivated us to launch  \texttt{AICY}: \href{https://aicy.physics.wisc.edu}{https://aicy.physics.wisc.edu}---living software and data repositories for toric Calabi-Yau manifolds. Existing databases for data mining Calabi-Yau manifolds such as the Kreuzer-Skarke database \cite{KS-Database} and the Altman database \cite{Altman-Database}, while documenting the state-of-the-art at a given point in time, are static. We envision \texttt{AICY} as a community-driven platform that integrates a software repository---featuring machine learning models like the transformer proposed in this work---that is self-improving through training on a continuously evolving data repository enriched by user contributions. \texttt{AICY} aims to provide a flexible framework for targeted Calabi-Yau search via reinforcement learning using user-defined reward functions, enabling adaptive and physics-informed exploration of the string landscape.

This paper is organized as follows. We begin in section~\ref{sec:CY} with a review of how Calabi-Yau manifolds arise from FRSTs of reflexive polytopes. Section~\ref{sec:databases} surveys existing databases and algorithms, with a particular focus on \texttt{CYTools}, from which we draw our models' training data.
In section~\ref{sec:machine_learning}, we detail our machine learning pipeline that allows transformers to learn the distribution of FRSTs and generate new ones.
We present our experimental setup in section~\ref{sec:experimental_setup}, and thoroughly study the training dynamics and generative power of our models in section~\ref{sec:results}. Finally, we detail our proposal for a community-driven platform in section~\ref{sec:AICY} and discuss future research directions in section~\ref{sec:dis}.

