\section{Calabi-Yau manifolds from reflexive polytopes via FRSTs}\label{sec:CY}
% \jy{rewrite in better order}\jy{references}

The task of generating FRSTs of reflexive polytopes arises from their role as sources of Calabi-Yau manifolds. Due to Batyrev~\cite{Batyrev:1993oya}, an FRST of a reflexive polytope $\Delta^\circ$ yields a sufficiently smooth toric variety that serves as the ambient space for a smooth Calabi-Yau hypersurface, defined by monomials corresponding to lattice points in the dual polytope $\Delta$. We provide in this section the necessary mathematical background for the construction.

\subsection{Reflexive polytopes}
A lattice polytope $\Delta$ is the convex hull of a set of vertices in a lattice $M$. Its dual $\Delta^\circ$ associated to the dual lattice $N$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
    \Delta^\circ={\rm{Conv}}(\{y\in N\mid y\cdot x\geq -1\;\forall x\in\Delta\}).
\end{equation}
If $\Delta^\circ$ is also a lattice polytope, then both $\Delta$ and $\Delta^\circ$ are \emph{reflexive} polytopes. A reflexive polytope has exactly one interior lattice point, which is taken to be the origin.

Reflexive polytopes serve as the primary input to Batyrev's construction. Reflexivity guarantees that the anticanonical divisor of the resulting toric variety is Cartier, so that the anticanonical hypersurface is globally defined and satisfies the Calabi-Yau condition of vanishing first Chern class via the adjunction formula. Morever, reflexivity ensures that $\Delta$ contains only finitely many lattice points, providing a finite basis of monomials that define the Calabi-Yau hypersurface.

Up to lattice automorphisms in $GL(n,\mathbb{Z})$, the number of reflexive polytopes in any given dimension $n$ is finite; e.g., there are only $16$ distinct reflexive polytopes in $2$ dimensions. Kreuzer and Skarke developed an algorithm to enumerate all reflexive polytopes for any $n$ \cite{Kreuzer:1998vb}. Of particular relevance to this work, they constructed a complete classification for $n=4$ \cite{Kreuzer:2000xy}, which contains $473{,}800{,}776$ polytopes from which as many as $10^{428}$ topologically inequivalent Calabi-Yau threefolds may be constructed~\cite{Demirtas:2020dbm}. This finite yet vast set of geometries enables large-scale combinatorial and statistical studies of the string landscape. 

\subsection{From FRSTs to desingularized, projective, and compact toric varieties}
A \emph{star} triangulation $\mathcal{T}$ of a reflexive polytope defines a simplicial fan $\Sigma$ consisting of cones $\sigma_i$. The star condition requires that every simplex in $\mathcal{T}$ contains the origin as a vertex, so that coning over these simplices yields a complete $\Sigma$ covering the entire $\mathbb{R}^n$, thereby ensuring the compactness of the associated toric variety and the Calabi-Yau hypersurface defined within it.

Via the Cox construction, the toric variety $\mathcal{A}(\Sigma)$ associated to a $\Sigma$ with $r$ rays ($1$-dimensional cones $\sigma_i(1)$) is given by
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{A}(\Sigma)=(\mathbb{C}^r-Z(\Sigma))/G,
\end{equation}
where $Z(\Sigma)$ is the exceptional set of $\Sigma$, and $G$ is the algebraic torus that imposes homogeneous scaling relations among the coordinates $z_i$ from the lattice vectors $v_i$ that generate $\sigma_i(1)$. Specifically, the torus group action identifies points under
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hcoor}
    (z_1,z_2,\dotsc,z_r)\sim(\lambda^{q_1} z_1, \lambda^{q_2} z_2,\dotsc,\lambda^{q_r} z_r)    
\end{equation}
for $\lambda\in\mathbb{C}-\{0\}$ and integers $q_i$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Geq}
    \sum_{i=1}^{r}q_iv_i=0.
\end{equation}
When the vector space containing $\Sigma$ is $n$-dimensional, \eqref{eq:Geq} has $r-n$ solutions. Therefore, $G$ is $(r-n)$-dimensional, and the dimension of $\mathcal{A}(\Sigma)$ is $r-(r-n)=n$.

Singularities in a toric variety arise when the cones in its associated fan are not smooth. A cone is not smooth if its set of minimal generators cannot be extended to a full $\mathbb{Z}$-basis of the lattice. These singularities may be inherited by the Calabi-Yau hypersurface to be defined inside the toric variety, rendering the hypersurface non-smooth. To resolve these singularities, the original fan must be refined by additionally requiring $\mathcal{T}$ to be \emph{fine}. Fineness requires that all maximal simplices in $\mathcal{T}$ are as subdivided as possible with respect to the lattice, so that the resulting fan has cones that are generated by elementary rays and are closer to being smooth. In other words, $\mathcal{T}$ should include all lattice points on the polytope, not just the vertices, as potential simplex vertices.

However, lattice points strictly interior to codimension-$1$ faces need not be included in $\mathcal{T}$. While omitting them may induce mild singularities in the toric variety, the Calabi-Yau hypersurface remains smooth, as the toric divisors corresponding to those points do not intersect a generic anticanonical Calabi-Yau hypersurface. This is known as the maximal projective crepant partial desingularization. The set of points consisting of all lattice points in the reflexive polytope, excluding those strictly interior to codimension-$1$ faces, together with the origin, is referred to as the set of \emph{resolved vertices}.

The final condition we impose on $\mathcal{T}$ is \emph{regularity} by requiring that it arises as the projection of the lower convex hull of lifted resolved vertices via a height function. The lifting procedure induces a piecewise linear convex function over the resolved vertices, which ensures that the associated fan is normal, and thus the resulting toric variety is projective and Kähler. The embedded Calabi-Yau hypersurface is thereby also projective and Kähler with finite-dimensional cohomology.

