To summarize, we seek \emph{fine, regular, and star triangulations} (FRSTs) of the resolved vertices of a reflexive polytope for constructing sufficiently desingularized, projective, and compact toric varieties:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Fine (F):} Every resolved vertex is a vertex of some simplex in $\mathcal{T}$; i.e., no resolved vertex is left unused.
    \item \textbf{Regular (R):} $\mathcal{T}$ arises as the projection of the lower faces of a convex polytope constructed from lifting each resolved vertex $p_i$ to $(p_i,h_i)\in\mathbb{Z}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}$ with some height $h_i\in\mathbb{R}$.
    \item \textbf{Star (S):} Every simplex in $\mathcal{T}$ contains the origin as a vertex.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Calabi-Yau manifolds}
Following the descriptions above, a sufficiently smooth toric fourfold $\mathcal{A}(\Sigma)$ can be constructed from the fan $\Sigma$ associated to an FRST of the resolved vertices of a reflexive polytope $\Delta^\circ$ in four dimensions. A smooth Calabi-Yau threefold $X\subset\mathcal{A}(\Sigma)$ is then realized as a generic anticanonical hypersurface embedded in the ambient toric fourfold, defined by
\begin{equation}
    \sum_{m\in\Delta\cap M}c_mp_m=0,
\end{equation}
where the coefficients $c_m$ specify the complex structure moduli of $X$, and $p_m$ is the monomial from the coordinates $z_i$
\begin{equation}
    p_m=\prod_{i=1}^{r}z_i^{v_i\cdot m+1}.
\end{equation}

A Calabi-Yau threefold can be characterized by two of its Hodge numbers, $h^{1,1}$ and $h^{2,1}$. They count ways in which the Kähler metric and the complex structure can be deformed, respectively. Mirror symmetry states that
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ms}
    h^{1,1}(X)=h^{2,1}(X^\circ)\;{\rm{and}}\;h^{1,1}(X^\circ)=h^{2,1}(X),
\end{equation}
where $X$ is constructed from $\Delta$, and $X^\circ$ from $\Delta^\circ$. Baytrev proved
\eqref{eq:ms} by providing the formulas \cite{Batyrev:1993oya}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:h11h21eqs}
\begin{split}
    h^{1,1}(X)&=\ell(\Delta^\circ)-4-1-\sum_{\Gamma^\circ}\ell^*(\Gamma^\circ)+\sum_{\Theta^\circ}\ell^*(\Theta^\circ)\ell^*(\hat{\Theta}^\circ)\\
    h^{2,1}(X)&=\ell(\Delta)-4-1-\sum_\Gamma\ell^*(\Gamma)+\sum_\Theta\ell^*(\Theta)\ell^*(\hat{\Theta}),
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\ell(\alpha)$ is the number of lattice points in $\alpha$, $\ell^*(\alpha)$ is the number of lattice points in the interior of $\alpha$, $\Gamma$'s ($\Gamma^\circ$'s) are codimension-$1$ faces of $\Delta$ ($\Delta^\circ$), $\Theta$'s ($\Theta^\circ$'s) are codimension-$2$ faces of $\Delta$ ($\Delta^\circ$), and $\hat{\Theta}$ ($\hat{\Theta}^\circ$) is the face of $\Delta^\circ$ ($\Delta$) dual to $\Theta$ ($\Theta^\circ$). \eqref{eq:ms} follows directly from the fact that $(\Delta^\circ)^\circ=\Delta$ for reflexive polytopes. 

The reflexive polytopes are primarily organized by $h^{1,1}$. As $h^{1,1}$ increases, enumerating all FRSTs of a polytope using non-learning algorithms becomes computationally prohibitive, since the number of resolved vertices typically grows, reaching up to 496 for a polytope with $h^{1,1}=491$. In this work, we consider triangulations only of \emph{favorable} reflexive polytopes, defined as those $\Delta^\circ$ for which the final term in the first equation of~\eqref{eq:h11h21eqs} vanishes. That is, the number of resolved vertices satisfies the relation $N_{\rm vert}=\ell(\Delta^\circ)-\sum_{\Gamma^\circ}\ell^*(\Gamma^\circ)=h^{1,1}+4+1$. For clarity to all audiences, we refer to each polytope configuration throughout the paper by its $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})$ tuple.

