\section{Triangulation software and a non-learning sampler}
\label{sec:databases}

\texttt{CYTools}~\cite{Demirtas:2022hqf} is a recently developed software package, with polytope triangulation as one of its primary design focuses. We describe how it is utilized in our setup below.

\subsection{Fetching reflexive polytopes}
The Kreuzer-Skarke (KS) database~\cite{Kreuzer:2000xy} contains the complete list of $473,800,776$ $4$D reflexive polytopes. Following Batyrev's prescription, this database enables the construction of companion databases of polytope FRSTs and the corresponding Calabi-Yau threefolds, such as Altman's~\cite{Altman:2014bfa}. In contrast, \texttt{CYTools} functions not as a static database but as a tool that fetches polytopes from the KS database and performs computations in real time.

\subsection{FRSTs enumeration}
\label{sec:all}
\texttt{CYTools} uses \texttt{TOPCOM} \cite{Topcom} as a backend to compute FRSTs of a given point set in Euclidean space. If the point set is not full-dimensional, an affine transformation is first applied to embed it into full-dimensional space. \texttt{TOPCOM} then enumerates all combinatorial FRSTs by exploring the connected subgraph, corresponding to regular triangulations, of the flip graph. In the full flip graph, each node represents a triangulation, and edges correspond to bistellar flips, which are local moves that replace a subcomplex with a complementary one sharing the same boundary. In other words, the set of FRSTs can be traversed via successive bistellar flips.

We use \texttt{CYTools} to enumerate complete sets of FRSTs in order to construct training sample sets for polytopes with configurations $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})=(5,9+1)$, $(6,10+1)$, $(7,11+1)$, and $(8,12+1)$. For polytopes with $N_{\rm vert}>12+1$, exhaustive enumeration becomes prohibitively slow and memory-intensive, as these polytopes admit too many triangulations. Full sets of FRSTs for selected test polytopes are also required for statistical evaluation (see section~\ref{subsec:perfmet}) of how representative the transformer-generated FRST samples are.

Notably, we have verified that for polytopes with $h^{1,1}=5$ and $6$, \texttt{CYTools} produces the same FRSTs as those stored in Altman's database. However, the polytopes, or more precisely, their resolved vertex sets, are expressed in different bases in the two sources. We find that the triangulations match only after transforming the resolved vertices into a normal form, as defined in~\cite{Kreuzer:1998vb}. This basis difference has implications for learning; our transformer model exhibits distinct loss curves when trained on data from Altman's database versus \texttt{CYTools}, suggesting that certain bases maybe more favorable for learning. We leave investigations of basis choice and its impact on training performance for future work.

\subsection{The fast sampler}
\label{sec:fast}
We describe a non-machine learning algorithm available in \texttt{CYTools}, the fast sampler, which provides a low-cost method for sampling FRSTs. It serves as a baseline for performance comparisons with our transformer model.

The regularity of a triangulation is guaranteed by assigning a height vector $\boldsymbol{h}$ that lifts the resolved vertices, such that the projection of the lower codimension-$1$ faces of the convex hull of the lifted vertices yields a regular triangulation. Choosing $h_i=\left|\boldsymbol{p}_i\right|^2$, where $\boldsymbol{p}_i$ denotes the coordinates of vertex $i$, produces the Delaunay triangulation.

FRSTs can be sampled around the Delaunay triangulation inexpensively using the following procedure:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Initialize the height vector as $h_i=\left|\boldsymbol{p}_i\right|^2$ for each resolved vertex $i$ of the given polytope.

    \item Sample $\epsilon_i$ for each vertex $i$ from a Gaussian distribution with standard deviation $c$, and update the height vector via $h_i\rightarrow h_i+\epsilon_i$.

    \item If the resulting triangulation is not fine, repeating the sampling and updating steps. Otherwise, lower the origin sufficiently to obtain an FRST.
\end{enumerate}

The parameter $c$ controls the extent of deviation from the Delaunay seed and thus governs the diversity of sampled triangulations. If $c$ is too large, some vertices may be lifted excessively, causing them to lie above the lower convex hull and leading to triangulations that are not fine. When the fast sampler is used as a baseline, we ensure that $c$ is carefully tuned to achieve optimal performance for fair comparisons.

In addition, we use the fast sampler to generate training samples for the $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})=(9,13+1)$ configuration. While computationally efficient, the fast sampler is not expected to yield fair sample distributions of geometric and topological quantities representative of the full population, as demonstrated in~\cite{Demirtas:2020dbm}.\footnote{In contrast, \texttt{CYTools} also provides a fair sampler, designed to yield more representative samples of FRSTs by more thoroughly exploring the height vector space and applying bistellar flips. However, for the small $(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert})$ polytopes considered in this work, the wall-finding step often fails persistently. Fine-tuning the sampler’s parameters on a per-polytope basis to improve its success rate lies beyond the scope of this study.}

