\section{Machine learning FRSTs with CYTransformer}\label{sec:machine_learning}
% \ca{Suggestion (with François' text as a basis, and meant to replace François' draft)}

Our goal is to train a model to take as input a reflexive polytope, represented by some encoding of its set of resolved vertices, and to output an FRST of this polytope; there should furthermore be some diversity and unbiasedness to the FRSTs thus produced. To do so, we use a vanilla encoder-decoder transformer---a simple yet amazingly flexible machine learning architecture. Our model, hereafter CYTransformer, takes a polytope as input, encodes it as a sequence of high-dimensional vectors (one per resolved vertex), and uses an auto-regressive decoder to output a sequence of discrete symbols, called \textit{tokens}. The sequence is produced token by token, until a special end-of-sequence token {\textlangle eos\textrangle} is generated. Each token (except a few special tokens) corresponds to a simplex of the input polytope, hence the resulting sequence represents a collection of simplices: we call it a \textit{candidate triangulation}. Sometimes CYTransformer ``fails'' and the sequence does not describe a FRST; in fact, it needs not even be a valid triangulation (as opposed to some unstructured collection of simplices). Consequently, the final step is to check whether the generated sequence describes a triangulation, and if so whether this triangulation is an FRST.

Our experiments consist of two phases: a training phase, and an inference phase. During the training phase, CYTransformer is presented with examples of polytopes with a fixed number of resolved vertices $N_\text{vert}$ and FRSTs of these polytopes drawn from \texttt{CYTools}, and learns to minimize a cross-entropy loss over the predicted tokens. This amounts to rewarding the model when it predicts sequences of simplices similar to the FRSTs from its training set. At the end of the training phase, CYTransformer has learned a conditional probability distribution of output tokens that allows it to predict new sequences of tokens for any input polytope with $N_\text{vert}$ resolved vertices; if the training has succeeded, these sequences should have a high probability of being FRSTs of the input polytope.

During the inference phase, the trained CYTransformer is used as a generator, to sample (from its learned conditional probability distribution) several candidate triangulations for a given polytope. These candidates are then tested using \texttt{CYTools} to check whether they are proper FRSTs. A well-trained model should generate a high proportion of diverse FRSTs. To ensure a fair evaluation, we make sure that there is no overlap between the sets of polytopes used to train our models and those on which we test the models' capacity for generating FRSTs.

This two-phase process can be iterated: the (correct) FRST produced by the model after an initial training phase can be collected in a new training set, which can be used to further train the model. This improved model can then generate additional FRSTs, which can serve as material for future finetuning phases; this process can be repeated. The goal of this iterated fine-tuning is to achieve a more diverse distribution of generated FRSTs than the one we started with, which is limited by the quality of the initial training set: we hope that at each inference step, the model generalizes beyond the training data that it has seen so far, and that this new, more diverse data can be used to further improve the model and diversify its output distribution.

