\subsection{Model architecture}

% \ca{Suggested text (based on François' draft)}

CYTransformer adopts the encoder-decoder transformer architecture introduced in the landmark paper~\cite{Vaswani:2017lxt}. While decoder-only models have become the norm in state-of-the-art large language models, our setting, in which the generation of the triangulation should be conditioned on the polytope, lends itself well to the addition of a bidirectional encoder\footnote{In particular, the encoder's bidirectionality might help produce a richer representation of the polytope, which could also be cached and reused in a more optimized setup.}. The high-level architecture, which we also illustrate in figure~\ref{fig:full_architecture}, is as follows: at any given step, the model takes as input the entire input polytope, encoded as a sequence of four-dimensional vectors representing its resolved vertices, and a partially constructed sequence of tokens. Each token represents a simplex, except for a few control tokens, such as the start of sequence token $\langle \text{sos} \rangle $. The input polytope is processed by the encoder to produce a sequence of high dimensional vectors: the encoder output. The encoder output, together with the sequence of tokens generated so far, are used as inputs by the decoder. The decoder then outputs a vector $\boldsymbol{P}$ representing a probability distribution over the set of all tokens considered; this distribution is conditioned by the encoder output, hence by the input polytope, and by the previously decoded tokens. At inference time, a new token is sampled from this probability distribution, and added to the sequence being constructed. This process is repeated until a special end of sequence token is produced, or until the sequence reaches a predefined length. Note that this means that the sequence generation procedure is non-deterministic: its stochasticity is in fact crucial to generating diverse FRSTs. At training time, the parameters of the encoder and the decoder are trained to minimize the cross-entropy loss between the vector $\boldsymbol{P}$ and the vector representing the Dirac distribution corresponding to the true next token in the training sequence.

