    \caption{The high level pipeline for our CYTransformer model, here represented in inference mode.
    }
    \label{fig:full_architecture}
    % \vspace{-1em}
\end{figure*}

At a more concrete level, our encoder is composed of a simple position-wise multi-layer perceptron, which maps each four-dimensional input vector to a high-dimensional vector, followed by a multi-layer, bi-directional transformer. Similarly, our decoder is composed of an embedding layer, which maps each token to a high-dimensional vector, followed by a multi-layer causal (or auto-regressive) transformer.
Each transformer layer consists of one attention layer and a multi-layer perceptron, with one hidden layer, known as the feed-forward network (FFN), built over a residual connection that copies the input sequence. The feed-forward network is a fully connected network with one hidden layer, and a hidden dimension four times\footnote{This ratio is a well-accepted convention in transformer architecture.} larger than the dimension of its input and output (i.e., the embedding dimension used throughout the transformer). The attention mechanism captures the dependencies between different elements in the sequence, by computing normalized dot products between the representations of the current token, and all others, transformed by learnable matrices known as keys, queries and values ($Q, K$, and $V$). In the encoder, the attention is bi-directional: each element is compared to all other elements in the sequence. The decoder, on the other hand, uses \emph{causal} attention, and only attends to the previous elements. The decoder layers also sport a second attention mechanism, known as cross-attention, which relates the decoded output to the encoder output. An illustration of an encoder and decoder transformer layers is given in figure~\ref{fig:encoder_decoder}. Further details can be found in the following subsections.

