\subsection{Inference}
Once a model has been trained on a training set of FRSTs and polytopes with $N_\text{vert}$ resolved vertices, it can be used to generate candidate triangulations for other polytopes with the same number of vertices.
We typically evaluate our models' capacities on a set of polytopes that are entirely distinct from those used during training: this is a fairer measure of performance, as it tests whether the model has truly captured the structure of FRSTs and can apply what it has learned to yet unseen polytopes, rather than simply memorized the examples seen during training.

Candidate triangulations are generated one token at a time. The encoder takes as input the set of non-zero vertices of a polytope. The decoder then takes the encoder output and an initial sequence of tokens of length $1$, namely [{\textlangle sos\textrangle}]. As explained earlier, the decoder outputs a probability distribution over the vocabulary of possible tokens, and a token (e.g. $\langle 21 \rangle$) is drawn from it and appended to the sequence (which becomes [{\textlangle sos\textrangle}, {$\langle 21 \rangle$}] in our example).
This new sequence, along with the input polytope, is then fed to the model again to generate yet another probability distribution, from which a new token is again sampled and added to the partially constructed sequence.
This process is repeated until the {\textlangle eos\textrangle} token is selected. We call the resulting sequence a \textit{candidate triangulation}. We verify whether it represents a triangulation meeting all the requirements for an FRST using \texttt{CYTools}\footnote{Specifically the function \texttt{p.triangulate()}, where \texttt{p} is a \texttt{Polytope} object constructed from the polytope vertices.}; if it does, we refer to the candidate triangulation as a ``transformer-generated FRST''.

We have considered other sampling procedures, and in particular beam search. Beam search is a widely used algorithm for finding the most probable output sequences. It keeps multiple ``beams'' active at all times and selects the most probable next tokens for each beam at every sampling step. However, preliminary experiments suggest that beam search is ineffective for our purpose of generating \emph{distinct} FRSTs, as the most probable beams have a tendency to differ only up to permutation and to represent the same FRST.\footnote{This behavior is not unexpected and is analogous to a language model generating answers with different wordings that are semantically equivalent.}
%Therefore, we use random sampling for the next token, naturally allowing less probable tokens to be selected and resulting in a more diverse set of candidate FRSTs. Each inference run takes approximately $0.3$ to $0.5$ seconds.\footnote{Also on a NVIDIA RTX A$4000$ GPU.}\jy{There should be an optimized way to maximize this diversity?shown by constant rate}



\subsection{Datasets and data splitting}\label{subsec:datasets}
To each $4$D reflexive polytope $\Delta$ corresponds the $h^{1,1}$ value of the Calabi-Yau manifolds that it generates (see section~\ref{sec:CY})--this quantity is of particular interest to physicists.
On the other hand, it is more reasonable from a machine learning perspective to classify polytopes $\Delta$ according to the numbers $N_{\rm{vert}}$ of resolved vertices of their dual polytope $\Delta^\circ$ (which is the one being triangulated), as this number conditions the length of the encoder input sequence. In particular, each model that we train is specialized for a specific value of $N_{\rm{vert}}$.
Consider the set $\mathcal{P}(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert})$ of all reflexive polytopes $\Delta$ corresponding to a given $h^{1,1}$ and such that their dual $\Delta^\circ$ has $N_{\rm vert}$ resolved vertices: we let $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert})$ be the set of all pairs $(\Delta^\circ, T)$ such that $\Delta \in \mathcal{P}(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert})$ and such that $T$ is an FRST of $\Delta^\circ$. We train and evaluate our models on (subsets of) such datasets $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert})$ for various values of $h^{1,1}$ and $N_{\rm vert}$.

For a given reflexive polytope, the two quantities $h^{1,1}$ and $N_{\rm{vert}}$ are loosely related through \eqref{eq:h11h21eqs}: for small values of $N_{\rm{vert}}$, we find that many, though not all, polytopes with $N_{\rm{vert}}$ resolved vertices are such that $N_{\rm vert}=h^{1,1}+5$. 
Out of convenience, we choose to restrict ourselves to polytopes such that $N_{\rm vert}=h^{1,1}+5$. More precisely, we consider in our experiments subsets of the datasets $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}=5, N_{\rm vert} = 9+1)$, $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}=6, N_{\rm vert} = 10+1)$, $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}=7, N_{\rm vert} = 11+1)$, $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}=8, N_{\rm vert} = 12+1)$ and $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}=9, N_{\rm vert} = 13+1)$.
Note that we write $N_{\rm vert} = a+1$ (for some value of $a$) to draw attention to the fact that the reflexive polytopes considered have $a$ non-zero resolved vertices, which appear in their encodings as inputs to the model, in addition to the origin, which does not.

We constitute our datasets using \texttt{CYTools}. When creating a training or test set for a given value of $(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert})$, we first pick $N_{\rm poly}$ polytopes in $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert})$ uniformly at random, then we pick $N_{\rm FRST}$ FRSTs for each polytope as follows: for $N_{\rm vert} \in \{9+1,10+1,11+1,12+1 \}$, the polytopes are small enough that \texttt{CYTools} is capable of enumerating all their triangulations; we can then pick triangulations uniformly at random among all the triangulations of the polytope considered. 
or $N_{\rm vert} = 13 + 1$, the polytopes are too large for \texttt{CYTools} to enumerate all their triangulations. Instead, we use \texttt{CYTools}' ``fast'' algorithm (see section~\ref{sec:databases}) to generate the desired number of triangulations per polytope.

