\section{Experimental setup}\label{sec:experimental_setup}
We describe the core elements of our experimental setup.
%Further details can be found in our code. \ca{Is this okay?}

\paragraph{Model hyperparameters}
We use the same model architecture for all of our experiments.
Our encoder and decoder transformers have similar architectures (except for their embedding layers): their internal dimension is $512$, they have $16$ layers, each layer uses $16$ heads. Each feedforward network has two layers and an internal dimension of $4\times 512$.
Our transformers have approximately $20$ million learnable parameters, though the precise number varies depending on $N_\text{vert}$.
This makes our models lightweight compared to state-of-the-art large language models, whose sizes range from $1$ billion to $1000$ billion parameters.
We expect larger models to yield even better performance, albeit at a higher computational cost.

\paragraph{Training}

During training, we use batches of size either $800$ or $1024$. The Adam optimizer is applied with a learning rate of $5\times10^{-5}$. We either let the learning rate be constant, or apply a mild decay coefficient of $0.8$ to it every $100,000$ steps. Through padding, we make each sequence used as an input to the decoder be of length $30$, $35$, $45$, $55$ and $65$ respectively when $N_{\text{vert}} = 9+1, 10+1, 11+1, 12+1$ and $13+1$ respectively (the length chosen is roughly that of the longest observed sequence for the value of $N_{\text{vert}}$ considered).
For each run, we define in advance a training set, a validation set on which a validation cross-entropy loss is computed for monitoring purposes, and a test set on which the trained model's FRSTs-generating capacities are tested.
We typically train until the model's performance starts to stagnate (see the next section for details on our performance metrics), which usually happens after $100,000 - 1,000,000$ 
 optimizer steps. This corresponds to a couple of days of training on $8$ Nvidia V100 GPUs (note that our setup is not optimized, and that much greater compute efficiency should be achievable). 

\paragraph{Regarding hyperparameters optimization}

We conducted preliminary experiments on the impact of various model hyperparameters, such as the models' internal dimension, number of heads, number of layers (for both encoder and decoder), as well as of various training hyperparameters, such as the learning rate, learning schedule and batch size.
In those experiments, we performed partial grid searches by letting a few related hyperparameters (such as the batch size and the learning rate) vary simultaneously and observed for each combination of hyperparameters the performances of the models thus trained.
While we do not claim that the hyperparameters that we ended up selecting (those described above) are optimal, they offered the best results within the constraints of our compute budget in those preliminary experiments.


\paragraph{Datasets}
Our datasets are generated as explained in subsection~\ref{subsec:datasets}.
For a given experimental run and unless otherwise specified, our validation sets, test sets and training sets (see the paragraph on Training above) contain the following numbers of polytopes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item For $N_{\text{vert}} = 9+1$: validation - $500$, test - $500$, training - between $1{,}000$ and $3{,}000$.
    \item For $N_{\text{vert}} = 10+1$: validation - $1{,}000$, test - $1{,}000$, training - between $2{,}000$ and $8{,}000$.
    \item For $N_{\text{vert}} = 11+1$: validation - $1,000$, test - $1{,}000$, training - between $2{,}000$ and $16{,}000$.
    \item For $N_{\text{vert}} = 12+1$: validation - $800$, test - $800$, training - between $2{,}500$ and $7{,}500$.
    \item For $N_{\text{vert}} = 13+1$: validation - $1{,}000$, test - $1{,}000$, training - between $3{,}000$ and $12{,}000$.
\end{itemize}
The training sets vary in size as we explore the impact of the amount of training data on the trained model's quality.
For small values of $N_{\text{vert}}$, the sizes of our datasets are limited by the comparatively small number of polytopes in $\mathcal{D}(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert})$  (e.g. roughly $5,000$ for $N_{\text{vert}} = 9+1$).   For larger values of $N_{\text{vert}}$, they are limited by computational costs.
The number of triangulations per polytope depends on the polytope. On average, polytopes have roughly $11$, $35$, $125$ and $505$ triangulations per polytopes when $N_{\text{vert}} = 9+1, 10+1, 11+1$ and $12+1$ respectively; for $N_{\text{vert}} = 13+1$, we only have access to (at most) $300$ triangulations per polytope (sampled with the ``fast'' algorithm mentioned above).
As for the number of polytopes, the number of triangulations per polytope in our training sets varies in our experiments (even for a given number of vertices) as we study the impact of the amount of training data.










