\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}


In this section, we present a comprehensive evaluation of CYTransformer and its derived methods. We begin by introducing the performance metrics used throughout, which assess both the efficiency of generating new FRSTs for unseen polytopes and the representativeness of the generated samples. We then evaluate CYTransformer in isolation, followed by a comparison with \texttt{CYTools}' fast sampling algorithm. Motivated by the observation that CYTransformer generates unbiased sets of FRSTs, we also propose a hybrid approach that integrates CYTransformer with the fast sampler to achieve both improved sample representativeness and high inference speed. Finally, we explore CYTransformer's self-improvement capability, demonstrating that retraining on its own high-quality outputs further enhances performance.

\subsection{Training metrics}
We monitor the training of our models as follows: every few thousand steps, we randomly pick $M$ polytopes in the test set, and let the the model being trained generate $N$ candidate triangulations for each of these polytopes (in practice, $N = M \in \{40, 80 \} $).
We then count the number of unique FRSTs among those $M\times N$ candidates. We call this number the \textit{FRST generation training accuracy}; while it does not capture every aspect of the model's performance, unlike the more refined metrics that we apply to our trained models, it is informative enough to be our primary training metric.
We also monitor the cross-entropy loss on the training and validation sets, as well as the number of unique star triangulations, fine star triangulations and regular star triangulations.


\subsection{Performance metrics}
\label{subsec:perfmet}
We use the following performance metrics to quantify the performance of our trained models.
Unless otherwise specified, all metrics are computed as averages over a set of candidate triangulations from $N_{\rm guess}=20{,}000$ inference runs per test polytope. The test set consists of $200$ randomly selected polytopes, fixed across models for each $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})$ configuration, and none of which are used during training. We consider the following statistics:
\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{FRST generation curve.} This primary performance metric plots the average number of valid FRSTs generated by the model as a function of $N_{\rm guess}$; it can be seen as a refined version of the FRST generation training accuracy used as a training metric.
    For each test polytope, the $N_{\rm guess}$ candidate triangulations are checked for validity as FRSTs, and a cumulative count is maintained. The curves are then averaged across all test polytopes. This metric reflects the model's raw ability to produce valid FRSTs, while the steepness of the curve indicates how efficiently the model lands in the FRST space with each inference call. We plot three variants of this curve:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \textbf{All FRSTs.} Counting all valid FRSTs, including duplicates if the same FRST is generated more than once.
        \item \textbf{Distinct FRSTs.} Counting only distinct FRSTs, which reflects the model's ability to discover new FRSTs.
        \item \textbf{NTFE FRSTs.} Counting only non-two-face-equivalent (NTFE) FRSTs, where candidate triangulations are compared against previously generated FRSTs under two-face equivalence. The NTFE curve captures the model’s ability to generate structurally distinct FRSTs, as measured by differences in two-face triangulations. Since TFE FRSTs yield topologically equivalent Calabi–Yau manifolds (by Wall's theorem \cite{WALL1966}), this metric serves as a proxy for the model’s capacity to explore beyond topologically redundant desingularizations.
    \end{enumerate}

    \item \textbf{FRST generation rate.} Defined as the FRST generation curve divided by $N_{\rm guess}$, this metric reflects how quickly valid FRSTs accumulate as sampling progresses. When only distinct FRSTs are counted, a high production rate indicates consistent discovery of new FRSTs, while a declining rate suggests increasing redundancy in the model's outputs.

    \item \textbf{FRST recovery curve.} This metric normalizes the FRST generation curve to reflect coverage of the full FRST space. For each test polytope, the cumulative number of distinct FRSTs found as a function of $N_{\rm guess}$ is divided by the total number of distinct FRSTs admitted by the test polytope.\footnote{The total number is computed by enumerating all FRSTs, as described in section~\ref{sec:all}, for polytopes with $N_{\rm vert}\leq12$.} The resulting curve captures how efficiently the model recovers the full space of FRSTs. As $N_{\rm guess}$ increases, the curve may approach a limiting value. Saturation well below 100\% indicates that the model fails to cover parts of the FRST space, regardless of sampling budget. Such poor coverage suggests mode collapse or an incomplete learned distribution of FRSTs. Comparing recovery curves across individual polytopes can reveal consistent failure modes tied to geometric features of the input polytopes. The average recovery curve over the test set is also reported.

    \item \textbf{Height vector similarity score histogram.} This metric explores how unbiasedly the model traverses the space of FRSTs. For each test polytope, we consider the set of FRSTs recovered by the model and compute a height vector $\boldsymbol{h} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\rm vert}}$ for each using \texttt{CYTools}. Height vectors can be seen as functions of the resolved vertices. Two height vectors $\boldsymbol{h}_1$ and $\boldsymbol{h}_2$ whose difference $\boldsymbol{h}_1 - \boldsymbol{h}_2 = \boldsymbol{h}_{\rm aff}\in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\rm vert}}$ is an affine function (affine with respect to the coordinates of the vertices) define the same triangulation, which makes the choice of height vector non-canonical. We remove this ambiguity by projecting each height vector onto the orthogonal complement of the subspace spanned by affine functions. The result is the affine-normalized height vector
    \begin{equation}
        \boldsymbol{h}_{\rm proj}=\boldsymbol{h}-\boldsymbol{h}_{\rm aff}=\boldsymbol{h}-\boldsymbol{Pc},
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{equation}
        \boldsymbol{P}=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        1 & \boldsymbol{p}^T_{1} \\
        1 & \boldsymbol{p}^T_{2} \\
        1 & \boldsymbol{p}^T_{3} \\
        \vdots & \vdots \\
        1 & \boldsymbol{p}^T_{N_{\rm vert}}
        \end{bmatrix}
    \end{equation} 
    encodes the vertex coordinates $\boldsymbol{p}_i$ and the vector $\boldsymbol{c}$ is an array of $5$ coefficients that solves the least square problem
    \begin{equation}
        \arg\min_{\boldsymbol{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{5}}|\boldsymbol{Pc}-\boldsymbol{h}|^2.
    \end{equation}
    This yields the solution $c=(\boldsymbol{P}^T\boldsymbol{P})^{-1}\boldsymbol{P}^T\boldsymbol{h}$. In addition, since scaling the height vector does not affect the resulting FRST, we must use a scale-invariant similarity metric. For example, the $\ell^2$-norm would undesirably penalize differences in overall magnitude. We therefore use cosine similarity between each projected height vector and that of the unique Delaunay triangulation, providing a scale-invariant comparison relative to a consistent reference. This yields a distribution of similarity scores for each test polytope. Comparing these distributions across models reveals how differently they sample the FRST space. In particular, comparing a model-generated distribution to the full population distribution computed from all distinct FRSTs allows us to assess how unbiasedly the model explores the FRST space relative to the total achievable diversity.
    % This metric explores how unbiasedly the model traverses the space of FRSTs. For each test polytope, we consider the set of FRSTs recovered by the model and compute a height vector $\boldsymbol{h}$ for each using \texttt{CYTools}. Since height vectors related \ca{related by addition ? maybe rephrase this} by an affine function $\boldsymbol{h}_{\rm aff}=\boldsymbol{Pc}$ \ca{$Pc$ not defined yet} define the same triangulation, we remove this ambiguity by projecting each height vector orthogonally to the affine subspace spanned by constant and linear functions on the vertex coordinates. The result is the affine-normalized height vector
