While training our models, we let them generate $1,600$ candidate triangulations ($40$ polytopes from the test set, $40$ candidates per polytope) every $50'000$ training steps, and count the number of unique FRSTs among those candidates.
We report in figure~\ref{fig:training_dynamics} the resulting FRST generation training accuracy curves for $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert}) \in \{(5, 9+1), (6,10+1), (7,11+1), (8,12+1) \}$.
Within a graph, each curve corresponds to a model trained on a different training set: we let the number of polytopes and the number of FRSTs per polytope vary to observe the impact of the training set on the models' performances. E.g., we consider for $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert}) = (5, 9+1)$ three training sets: one with $2,000$ polytopes and up to $2$ FRSTs per polytope (possibly less if the polytope does not admit $2$ FRSTs), one with $2,000$ polytopes and up to $6$ FRSTs per polytope, and one with $3,000$ polytopes and all of their FRSTs. We denote them as $(2000,2)$, $(2000,6)$ and $(3000,\text{all})$ in the graph's legend.

We see that our models successfully learn the structure of FRSTs and are capable of generalizing to polytopes that were not part of their training sets, while maintaining a diverse output distribution: our best models can generate up to $1,100$ unique FRSTs out of $1,600$ candidates.
We observe, in line with standard machine learning wisdom, that larger training sets tend to result in greater performances.
We also see that increasing $N_{\text{vert}}$ increases the difficulty of the task: models keep improving after $800,000$ training steps for $(8, 12+1)$, while they stagnate after $100,000$ for $(5, 9+1)$.
Note that the comparatively low number of unique FRSTs produced for $(5, 9+1)$ is explained by the small number of FRSTs that each polytope admits, rather than by the difficulty of generating them.
We also monitor the percentages of star triangulations, fine star triangulations and regular star triangulations among the generated candidates (not reported in the figure). We observe that that those percentages remain negligible at all time--in other words, models do not first learn to output star triangulations, then to output FRSTs, but rather directly learn the FRSTs distribution.
We pick for each case $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})$ the model that has achieved the highest FRST generation training accuracy over training (and at the training step where this accuracy was achieved).
In what follows, we refer to these selected models as CYTransformers, and analyze their performance more in depth.





\subsection{CYTransformer sampling performance}



This subsection evaluates trained instances of CYTransformer in isolation, focusing on both the efficiency and representativeness of their FRST sampling.

\textbf{Efficiency.} Figure~\ref{fig:solo_ps} shows the FRST generation curves produced by CYTransformer across polytopes with $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})=(5,9+1)$ to $(8,12+1)$. All three variants of the curve (counting all, distinct, and NTFE FRSTs) are plotted, with the corresponding generation rates shown below each. For clarity, the plots are restricted to the first $1{,}000$ candidate triangulations, where early-time model behavior is most visible.

The generation rate for all FRSTs remains nearly constant, reflecting the independence of candidate triangulations and the fact that the model has a fixed probability of producing a valid FRST on each inference attempt. In contrast, the generation rate for distinct FRSTs drops as $N_{\rm guess}$ increases. This behavior is expected because once the most common FRSTs are discovered, the model must sample rarer ones, leading to diminishing returns.

For polytopes with smaller $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})$, and hence fewer FRSTs, the distinct generation rate drops off quickly, indicating that the model rapidly saturates the available FRST space. As $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})$ increases, the size and complexity of the FRST space grow, and the rate decays more gradually. The model maintains a nontrivial generation rate over hundreds of candidate triangulations, suggesting sustained efficiency in discovering new FRSTs. Even at $N_{\rm guess}=1{,}000$, the rate does not fall to zero, indicating continued exploration of more difficult or infrequent regions of the FRST space. In practice, this behavior makes CYTransformer a reliable FRST sampler. Users can expect a steady return of new triangulations across extended sampling runs.

