\subsection{CYTransformer-seeded fast sampler}
This subsection presents a natural extension of the findings in the previous subsection. Having established that CYTransformer acts as a fair global explorer of the FRST space, while the fast sampler serves as an efficient local scanner, their complementary strengths motivate a hybrid strategy that combines both models: the CYTransformer-seeded fast sampler.

The method is implemented as follows. We first run CYTransformer for $N_{\rm cut}$ inference steps and collect all distinct FRSTs generated during this stage. These FRSTs form a seed pool. For the remaining $20{,}000-N_{\rm cut}$ candidate triangulations, we sample a seed uniformly at random from this pool and use it to initialize the fast sampler. The fast sampler then performs a single local perturbation, producing a new candidate triangulation.\footnote{As before, we have tuned the standard deviation of the Gaussian noise in the fast sampler to optimize the resulting recovery curves.} As a proof of concept, we focus on the $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})=(8,12+1)$ configuration to evaluate the effectiveness of this hybrid approach. In figure~\ref{fig:combined_recovery}, we compare recovery curves for distinct and NTFE FRSTs across pure CYTransformer, pure fast sampler, and various hybrid strategies with different values of $N_{\rm cut}$. Each cross marker indicates where the transformer is stopped, and the corresponding curve extending from the marker shows the performance of the hybrid method for the remaining candidate triangulations.

We find that when $N_{\rm cut}=100$, the hybrid method underperforms relative to CYTransformer alone, likely because the seed pool is too small to adequately represent the FRST space. As $N_{\rm cut}$ increases to $500$, the hybrid method matches CYTransformer’s performance. Further increases to $2{,}000$, $5{,}000$, and $10{,}000$ lead to hybrid curves that outperform pure CYTransformer, indicating that the fast sampler effectively leverages the growing diversity of the seed pool to discover new FRSTs. However, the gain appears to saturate as expected with increasing $N_{\rm cut}$, suggesting diminishing returns once the seed pool becomes sufficiently representative. We observe similar trends in the NTFE recovery curves.

These results reinforce our earlier conclusion that the fast sampler is a highly efficient local scanner. When seeded with CYTransformer-generated samples which are unbiased across the FRST space, the fast sampler is able to efficiently exhaust nearby triangulations that CYTransformer may overlook. Notably, the case with $N_{\rm cut}=500$ performs comparably to the full CYTransformer run, indicating that computational savings can be achieved without sacrificing recovery efficiency. Since transformer inference is more computationally expensive than fast sampling, the hybrid approach enables early stopping of CYTransformer and continuation with the cheaper fast sampler, making the overall generation process more cost-effective.

