\subsection{Self-improvement capability}
% \ca{Done, check if ok (I kept it humble, considering the circumstances)}
We have seen in subsection~\ref{subsec:training_dynamics} that the size of the training set is positively correlated with the performance of the trained model.
However, generating training data using \texttt{CYTools}' sampling algorithms becomes harder and more costly as $N_{\text{vert}}$ increases.
We explore a form of reinforcement learning, which we simply call self-improvement, as a solution to this limitation. The core idea is to use the model's own generated FRSTs for retraining.
More specifically, we start by training CYTransformer on a given training set (which could be small).
After a certain number of training steps, the training is paused and we let the model generate candidate triangulations for polytopes drawn from the training set\footnote{Note that there is no test set contamination here, as this is still part of the training procedure.}.
Valid FRSTs among these candidates are identified and added back to the training set, thus expanding it. The model is then retrained for another epoch, and this cycle is repeated iteratively.
As the model generates new training data for itself, this approach may require only a modest amount of initial data to yield a powerful trained model.

We test the self-improvement procedure in the case $(h^{1,1}, N_{\rm vert}) = (6,10+1)$, with an initial training set of size $(8{,}000,5)$, i.e., $8{,}000$ polytopes with only $5$ triangulations per polytope. Every $50{,}000$ steps, we pause the training to generate $480$ candidate triangulations for each of the $8{,}000$ polytopes of the training set, evaluate them and include the valid FRSTs among them to the training set.
We observe that performance stagnates after the first retraining iteration (after $100{,}000$ steps), and report in figure~\ref{fig:self_improvement_recovery_curves} the FRST recovery curves of the self-improved model after $50{,}000$ steps and $100{,}000$ steps.
We see that self-improvement does result in an increase in model performance, by approximately $25\%$ in terms of the average number of distinct FRSTs recovered at $N_{\rm guess}=20{,}000$. 
We leave to future work a more thorough exploration of the self-improvement procedure, particularly in terms of hyperparameters (frequency of the training set updates, number of candidates generated at each update, etc.).


