\textbf{Code and hyperparameters optimization.} 
While our models already achieve a satisfying level of performance, this work is chiefly exploratory, and our code, hyperparameters selection and training procedures could be further optimized.
In particular, larger models are expected to yield higher performances, while the addition of advanced machine learning techniques, such as key-value caching, could considerably speed up training.

\textbf{Priming.} A key strength of the transformer model is its capacity to sample fairly across the FRST space. This ability underpins its effectiveness and motivates its use in hybrid methods, such as the transformer-seeded fast sampler proposed in this paper. We expect this global, unbiased behavior to become even more beneficial as we move to larger polytopes with increasingly complex FRST spaces. However, practical constraints may hinder this advantage. For instance, for polytopes with $N_{\rm vert}>12+1$, it becomes marginally prohibitive to enumerate all FRSTs of each polytope. In such cases, we must rely on approximate methods, like the fast sampler, to generate training data. Since the fast sampler is biased by design, this introduces the concern that the transformer, trained on such biased data, may also become biased during inference.\footnote{Fortunately, we find this not to be the case for $(h^{1,1},N_{\rm vert})=(9,13+1)$, for which we observe preliminary success. Full results will be reported in future work.}

To retain the model’s strength even when training on biased data, the transformer must learn the underlying rules of triangulation, rather than merely memorize patterns. We propose using a technique known as priming, originally introduced to support sequence length generalization in transformers, as a strategy for this purpose. In our context, priming involves retraining the model with a small number of out-of-distribution samples from larger polytopes, encouraging generalization from previously learned, smaller cases. Success in this approach would indicate that the model is learning transferrable knowledge, namely, the structural principles of FRSTs, rather than overfitting to specific examples. This is a major advantage over non-learning methods like the fast sampler, which lack this transferability.

When combined with the self-improvement strategy, priming offers a pathway for iterative bootstrapping to larger configurations. Starting with a transformer trained on a modest $(h^*,N^*_{\rm vert})$ configuration, one can introduce a small priming set with $(h^*+1, N^*_{\rm vert}+1)$ and use it to retrain the model. After applying self-improvement via transformer-generated FRSTs, the cycle can repeat at the next size. \texttt{AICY} is designed to automate this process continuously on the server side.

\textbf{Targeted search.} A key future application of our transformer framework is enabling targeted searches for Calabi-Yau manifolds with desired properties. To accommodate this, we envision a general-purpose pipeline in which users simply supply a callable function that computes the properties of interest from a FRST. Our framework integrates this function seamlessly into the training or inference process. This flexible design supports optimization for a wide range of use cases without requiring any modification to the underlying model or architecture. Targeted search algorithms such as genetic algorithms 
and reinforcement learning \cite{Cole:2019enn,Krippendorf:2021uxu,Cole:2021nnt,Abel:2021ddu,MacFadden:2024him,Berglund:2024reu}
that have been used for navigating the landscape can be readily integrated into the \texttt{AICY} framework.

Two complementary strategies can be implemented for targeted search. The first is supervised fine-tuning, where the transformer is further trained on a curated subset of FRSTs that yield desirable properties in the derived Calabi-Yau manifolds. This subset is labeled by applying the user-supplied function in \texttt{AICY}'s FRST database. Retraining on this labeled dataset biases the model towards sampling the desired FRSTs. The second strategy is reinforcement learning, which removes the need for preprocessing. Instead, the transformer is trained via policy gradient methods, using the user-supplied function as a reward signal. The model then is guided to explore and discover FRSTs that optimize the specified properties. These two approaches complement each other, as supervised fine-tuning benefits from known examples, while reinforcement learning enables exploration beyond the labeled regime, allowing for out-of-distribution learning. 

\textbf{Tokenization.}
We adopted a general-purpose tokenization scheme that allows arbitrary triangulations to be represented, enabling broad applicability. However, more specialized tokenizations may simplify specific tasks. For example, using the height vector representation guarantees regularity by construction, with tokens corresponding directly to height values. Another possibility is to design token sequences that encode specific structural features, such as NTFE constraints, by explicitly including two-face configurations in the input. While specialized schemes can improve performance on targeted tasks, we chose to emphasize generality in this work to demonstrate the versatility of the transformer approach.

\textbf{Inference algorithm.} Despite the transformer's high sampling efficiency in terms of $N_{\rm guess}$, each inference step is computationally expensive compared to lightweight methods like the fast sampler. This cost could be reduced by making inference steps stateful rather than independent. For example, the model could track previous candidate triangulations during inference and dynamically adjust token sampling probabilities to avoid duplications and promote diversity. Such improvements could significantly enhance inference efficiency without retraining.

\textbf{Trained model as a database.} Given the vast number of FRSTs across all $4$D reflexive polytopes, it is infeasible to store them all in a static database. Instead, a well-trained transformer model that samples fairly from the FRST space offers a dynamic alternative. Rather than retrieving from a finite database, one queries the model to generate triangulations on demand.

