\subsection{Inferring known FRSTs: $(h^{1,1}=5,N_{\rm vert}=10)$ and $(h^{1,1}=6, N_{\rm vert}=11)$}
\jy{Use the term $N_\text{guess}$.}

In each case, we use the trained model to generate $N_\text{guess}$ candidate FRSTs for each input polytope in the test set. For both cases, we set $N_\text{guess}=200$ to facilitate comparisons. In general, the number of FRSTs for a polytope is expected to scale factorially with $N_\text{vert}$, and $N_\text{guess}$ can be set to scale with $N_\text{vert}$ accordingly. This is particularly relevant if the inference efficiency, defined as the average number of transformer-generated FRSTs per candidate FRST, remains consistent as $N_\text{vert}$ increases. However, it is not always necessary to exhaust the possible FRSTs for every polytope, as that would be infeasible due to combinatorial explosion. Furthermore, using a large $N_\text{guess}$ leads to significant waste of computational resources on polytopes with a small number of possible FRSTs,\footnote{Moreover, it is unclear how to estimate the number of possible FRSTs for a given polytope without explicitly determining them.} since most candidate FRSTs would be equivalent. Therefore, we recommend that practically $N_\text{guess}$ be determined based on the number of polytopes available and the required number of FRSTs.

Our vision is to create a platform that integrates software for our trained transformers with an online database of transformer-generated FRSTs. Users will perform inference with our transformers using their local computational resources, and any newly generated FRSTs will contribute to the online database. This approach allows us to pool computational power across the community. Additionally, the continuously updated database provides fresh training samples for further improving the transformers through expert iteration.

