\subsection{Gaussian trick}
\label{app:Gaussian_trick}

We consider a noisy Gaussian random variable: 
\begin{eqnarray}
    Y=X+\epsilon, \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
where $X\sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$ and $\epsilon$ a random noise.
\\\\

To better reconstruct the distribution of $X$ from $Y$, we consider $\Tilde{X}$ as a proxy of $X$ defined as follows:
\begin{eqnarray}
    \Tilde{X}:=F^{-1}\left(F_y\left(Y\right)\right),
\end{eqnarray}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $F(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^x e^{-\frac{t^2}{2}}{\rm d}t$ the distribution function of a standard Gaussian random variable;
    \item $F_y(x)=\Prob\left(Y\leq x\right)$ the distribution function of $Y$.
\end{itemize}
