\subsection{Empirical distributions of $\beta$ and $\sigma$ entries}
\label{app:betasigmaemiricaldistrib}
In order to build an intuition of the distribution to consider in the data generating process of the numerical experiments, we conserved the calibrated exposure vector related to the S\&P500 and plotted the empirical distribution of his entries. We found out that a leptokurtic distribution fits well the observed histogram for instance a bêta distribution of parameters respectively $9.66$ and $5.63$.
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{histogramExposures.pdf} % Replace with your figure
        \label{fig:subfig1}
    \caption{The empirical distribution of the $\beta$ entries (\textbf{blue}) calibrated on the returns of 300 arbitrary S\&P500 stocks, together with the fitted bêta distribution of parameters respectively $9.66$ and $5.63$ (\textbf{red}).}
    \label{fig:main}
\end{figure}
On the other hand, to build an intuition on the empirical distributions of $\sigma$ entries, we conduct a similar analysis by regressing the asset returns of the S\&P500 stocks and retrieve the calibrated entries of $\sigma$ through the identification of the variance of the residuals.
It turns out that they fit a leptokurtic distribution as well, a bêta distribution of parameters respectively $13.1$ and $4.18$ for example.
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{histogramsigma.pdf} % Replace with your figure
        \label{fig:subfig1}
    \caption{The empirical distribution of the $\sigma$ entries (\textbf{blue}) calibrated on the returns of 300 arbitrary S\&P500 stocks, together with the fitted bêta distribution of parameters respectively $13.10$ and $4.18$ (\textbf{red}).}
    \label{fig:main}
\end{figure}
