\title[The Schur multiplier norm and its dual norm]{\textsc{The Schur multiplier norm and  its dual norm}}



\author[E.~Christensen]{Erik Christensen}
\address{\hskip-\parindent
Erik Christensen, Mathematics Institute, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark.}
\email{echris@math.ku.dk}
\date{\today}
\subjclass[2010]{ Primary: 15A39, 15A60, 46L07. Secondary: 15A45, 47A30, 81P47.}
\keywords{Schur Product, Hadamard product, multiplier norm, diagonal of a matrix, trace of a matrix, order on matrices, completely bounded norm, bilinear forms}  

\begin{abstract}  For a complex self-adjoint  $n \times n $ matrix $X$ we show that its Schur multiplier norm is determined by $$ \|X\|_S = \min \{\, \|\mathrm{diag}(P)\|_\infty \, :\,   - P \leq X \leq P \, \}.$$
 The dual space of $( M_n(\bc), \|.\|_S)$ is  $(M_n(\bc), \|.\|_{cbB}).$ For  $X=X^*:$  $$ \|X\|_{cbB} = \min \{ \, \mathrm{Tr}_n\big(\Delta(\lambda)\big)\, :\, \lambda \in \br^n, \, - \Delta(\lambda) \leq  X  \leq \Delta(\lambda)\,\}. $$   
 \end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Issai Schur studied in  \cite{Sc} the entry wise product of two scalar  $ n \times n $ matrices $(X \circ Y)_{(i,j)}:= X_{(i,j)}Y_{(i,j)}$ and he looked at the multiplier norm - now the Schur multiplier norm $\|X\|_S$ -   where the  factor $X$ is fixed and $Y$  in $M_n(\bc)$ is equipped with the operator norm, $\|Y\|_\infty.$   The following theorem is a reformulation  of {\em Satz V} in Schur's article. 
\begin{theorem} \label{Schur}
Let $X$  be a positive complex $n \times n$ matrix, then $\|X\|_S = \|\mathrm{diag}(X)\|_\infty.$ 
\end{theorem} 
This is a remarkable simple formula, and here we  present, in Theorem \ref{ScSa}, an extension of this formula to the case of self-adjoint matrices. The new formula is based on the concept of order on self-adjoint matrices and norms of diagonals. In the case of a positive matrix we just get Schur's theorem back. 
On the other hand our result is very closely related  to previous results by Haagerup \cite{UH}, and an extension of his results by Ando and Okubo \cite{AO}, but we think, that our version of their findings is closer  to the original formulation of Schur's theorem.

While we were studying some aspects of the theory of completely bounded multilinear maps in \cite{C3}, we realized that for any pair of integers $m, n$ the vector space $M_{(m,n)}(\bc)$ of complex $m \times n$ matrices, equipped with the Schur multiplier norm $\|.\|_S,$ has a dual space which isometrically may be identified with the space $M_{(m,n)}(\bc) $ equipped with the norm, we have denoted $\|.\|_{cbB}.$ The reason for this notation is that for a matrix $X,$ the norm $\|X\|_{cbB} $ is defined as the completely bounded norm of the bilinear form on $\bc^m \times \bc^n$ which has the kernel $X.$ To see that you shall look at $\bc^k$ as a $k-$dimensional abelian C*-algebra.  
The completely bounded norm of a multilinear map on a pair of C*-algebras with values in an operator space, was defined in \cite{CS}, and its properties are described in \cite{Pa}. 

The second formula mentioned in the abstract was proven in  \cite{C3} for positive matrices and it turned out, while realizing how Schur's result could be generalized to self-adjoint matrices, that a similar extension also was possible in the dual case. The duality just mentioned may be seen as a shadow of the well known duality between an $\ell^\infty-$norm, played by the $\|.\|_S$ and an $\ell^1-$norm played by $\|.\|_{cbB}.$  

\smallskip

In the theory of Fourier  Analysis it is well known that there is a duality which involves the two products named the {\em convolution product } and the {\em point-wise product} of two continuous $2\pi-$periodic complex functions. In Schur's article he uses the name {\em convolution product} for the product on matrices, which today is named {\em the matrix product.}   It was probably clear to John von Neumann, that the so-called  {\em group measure space construction of a von Neumann algebra, } which now is called a crossed product of an abelian C*-algebra by a discrete group, provides a way to see how Schur's product on the complex $n \times n$ matrices actually may be viewed as a convolution product on a algebra whose elements are functions on the abelian cyclic group $\cc_n$ of order $n $ with values in diagonal $n \times n$ scalar matrices. This point of view is closely related to  Haamard's  work \cite{Ha} on the product on Laurent series given as the entry-wise product of the coefficients. We think that this is the reason why John von Neumann used the name {\em Hadamard product} for the product we have chosen to name after Schur. Several  authors use the word  {\em Hadamard product,} and R. A. Horn has written a very informative and readable  exposition \cite{Ho} with the title {\em The Hadamard product.}  The details in how we see the {\em Schur product}  as a {\em Hadamard product}  may be found in Section 4 of \cite{C5}. The close connection between  the Schur product and its analogous  convolution product in  group algebras and crossed product algebras has been known for long, but we will only use a small part of this theory. We quote Theorem 5.1 in Pisier's book  \cite{Pi2} or  Theorem 8.7 in Paulsen's book \cite{Pa} 
\begin{theorem}
Let $X$ be a complex  $m \times n$ matrix. The Schur multiplier norm $\|X\|_S $ is  at most 1 if and only if there exists a Hilbert space $K,$ with vectors $\{\xi_1, \dots , \xi_m\},  \, \{\eta_1, \dots, \eta_n\}$ all of length at most 1 such that $\forall\,  i, j : X_{(i,j)} = \langle \eta_j, \xi_i\rangle.$ 
\end{theorem}  
It is clear that you may assume that the dimension, say $d$ of $K$ may be assumed to be finite and at most equal to $m +n.$ Then if you chose an orthonormal basis $\{\d_1, \dots, \d_d\}$ for $K,$ the set of vectors $\{\xi_1, \dots , \xi_m\},  \, \{\eta_1, \dots, \eta_n\}$ may be written as a couple of matrices $L$ in
 $M_{(d,m)}(\bc)$ and $R$ in $M_{(d,n)}(\bc)$ such that $X = R^*L$ and the column norm of both $L$ and $R$ is at most 1. The column norm of a matrix $X$ is denoted $\|X\|_c$ and it is  the maximum over the norms of the columns in the matrix. Then we have the following statement, Theorem 2.7 of \cite{C3}.

 \begin{theorem} \label{L*R}
 Let $X$ be a complex $m \times n$ matrix, the Schur multiplier norm, $\|X\|_S,$ of $X$ is given by
 \begin{align*} \|X\|_S = \min\{\| L\|_c \|R\|_c\, :&\, d \in \bn,\, L \in M_{(d,m)}(\bc), R \in M_{(d,n)}(\bc),\\ &X = L^*R\,\}.\end{align*} 
 The minimum is attained for any $d \geq  \mathrm{rank}(X).$ 
 \end{theorem}
 The space $M_{(m,n)}(\bc)$ has the inner product 
 $$ \langle X, Y \rangle := \mathrm{Tr}_n(Y^*X),$$ and this inner product gives rise to a realization of the dual space to the Banach space $(M_{(m,n)}(\bc),\|.\|_S)$ via a new norm on on $M_{(m,n)}(\bc),$  which we denote $\|X\|_{cbB} $  and we define by by the formula  
 \begin{align} \label{cbB}
& \forall X \in M_{(m,n)}(\bc)  : \\ \notag &\|X\|_{cbB}:= \sup\{|\mathrm{Tr}_n(Y^*X)|\,  : \, Y \in M_{(m,n)}(\bc), \, \|Y \|_S \leq 1\,\}.
 \end{align}
 The reason for the suffix {\em cbB} is explained 
 above, and we  will not need the theory of completely bounded maps here, but just mention that the cbB-norm plays an important role in our recent research on some points of view on Grothendieck's inequalities, \cite{Gr}, \cite{Pi2}, \cite{C6}.
  The language in those articles is somewhat different from the standard one used in the study of Grothendieck's inequalities, so what we presented in our previous articles is not entirely original, but  rather an exposition, which in our situation, with complex matrices,  fits nicely with the set up  provided by the theory of completely bounded maps \cite{Pa}  and operator spaces \cite{Pi1}. We gave in Theorem 2.4 of \cite{C3} a description of the  cbB-norm as a factorization norm, which we will need here.  In \cite{C4} Corollary 2.2 we extended the factorization result with some uniqueness statements. To explain that, we will introduce some notation. For a vector $\g$ in $\bc^k$ we let $\Delta(\g)$ denote the diagonal $k \times k$ matrix with entries from $\g,$ and we will let $\|X\|_\infty$ denote the operator norm of the matrix $X$ then:
 \begin{theorem} \label{cbBNorm} 
 Let $X$ be in $M_{(m,n)}(\bc)$ then 
 \begin{align*} \|X\|_{cbB}  = \min \{\|B\|_\infty\, :& \, B \in M_{(m,n)}(\bc), \eta \in \bc^m, \eta_i \geq 0, \|\eta\|_2 =  1, \\ \notag &\xi \in \bc^n, \xi_j \geq 0, \|\xi\|_2 =1,  X = \Delta(\eta) B \Delta(\xi)\} \end{align*} 

 A factorization with $\|B\|_\infty = \|X\|_{cbB} $ is unique if the support of $B$ is contained in the support  of $\Delta(\xi)$ and the range of $B$ is contained in the support of $\Delta(\eta).$ 

 If $X$ is a self-adjoint matrix then $\eta = \xi $ and $B=B^*$ in the unique cbB-factorization. 
 \end{theorem}
 It turned out in \cite{C4} Theorem 3.5 that for a positive matrix $X$ there exists a formula for the norm $\|X\|_{cbB} $ which is based on the order   on matrices and properties of diagonal operators, as in the case of the Schur norm of a positive operator. 
 \begin{theorem} \label{cbBpos}
 Let $X$ be a positive complex $n \times n $ matrix then $$\|X\|_{cbB} = \min \{\mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\l))\, : \, \l \in \bc^n, \, \Delta(\l) \geq X\,\}.$$ 
\end{theorem}  

\smallskip

  In this article we will extend the theorems \ref{Schur} and \ref{cbBpos} in the following ways. 
 \begin{theorem} \label{ScSa}
 Let  $X$ be a complex  self-adjoint $n \times n$ matrix and $P$ a positive $n \times n$ matrix. If $-P \leq X \leq P$ then $\|X\|_S \leq \|P\|_S.$ 
 There exists a positive matrix $P$ and a self-adjoint contraction matrix $E $ such that  $X= \sqrt{P}E\sqrt{P} $ and $\|X\|_S = \|P\|_S.$  
 \end{theorem}

 The theorem has a corollary, which is a weaker statement, but in some circumstances  easier to recall and use. 

 \begin{corollary} \label{ScCor}
 A complex sel-adjoint $n
 \times n $ matrix $X$ has Schur multiplier norm at most 1 if and only if it may be written as $X = A - B$ with $A \geq 0, $ $B \geq 0$ and $\|A+B\|_S \leq 1.$ \end{corollary} 


 \begin{theorem} \label{cbBSa}
  Let  $X$ be a complex self-adjoint $n \times n$ matrix and $\Delta(\lambda)$ a positive diagonal matrix. If  $- \Delta(\lambda) \leq X \leq \Delta(\lambda) $ then $\|X\|_{cbB}  \leq \mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\lambda)).$ 
  There exists a unique positive diagonal matrix $\Delta(\lambda)$ and a self-adjoint contraction matrix $B$ such that  $X= \sqrt{\Delta(\lambda)}B\sqrt{\Delta(\lambda)}$ and $\mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\lambda)) = \|X\|_{cbB} .$ If the support of $B$ is dominated by the support of $\Delta( \l)$ then $B$ is uniquely determined. 
 \end{theorem}
 You may wonder if an optimal matrix $P$ from Theorem \ref{ScSa} also might be uniquely determined, but this   is not the case, as the following  example shows \begin{equation} \notag \begin{pmatrix}
 1&0\\0&-\frac{1}{4} \end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}
 1&0\\0&1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
 1&0\\0&-\frac{1}{4} \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
 1&0\\0&1 \end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}
 1&0\\0&\frac{1}{2} \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
 1&0\\0&-1 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
 1&0\\0&\frac{1}{2} \end{pmatrix}. 
 \end{equation} 
 This example demonstrates an $\ell^\infty-$norm aspect of the Schur norm. 


 In the general case where $X$ is a complex $m \times n$  matrix we use the standard trick and apply the theorems 
\ref{ScSa} and \ref{cbBSa} to the self-adjoint complex $(m+n) \times (m+n)$ matrix $Y$ given by $$ Y:= \begin{pmatrix}
0 & X\\ X^* &0
\end{pmatrix},$$ and in this way we do get some new insights into the  possible ways to estimate the norms $\|X \|_S $ and $\|X\|_{cbB}.$ We will present our findings in Section \ref{GenCase}. 

In Section \ref{LP} we transform the statement in Theorem \ref{cbBSa} into a question of solving a {\em linear program} $(\cp)$ and then we show that the dual program and its solution is closely connected to the duality between the cbB-norm and the Schur multiplier norm. 

\section{Proofs of the theorems \ref{ScSa} and \ref{cbBSa}}
\subsection{Poof of Theorem \ref{ScSa}}
\begin{proof}
 Suppose $-P \leq X \leq P$ then we can define positive matrices $A$ and $B$ by 
 \begin{equation}
 A := \frac{1}{2}(P+X),\quad B := \frac{1}{2}(P-X), \text{ so } P = A+B, \quad X = A-B.
\end{equation}  
 Let $Q$ denote the range projection of $P,$ then there exist matrices $a $ and $b$ in $M_n(\bc)$ such that$$ \sqrt{A} = a\sqrt{P}, \,\sqrt{B} = b\sqrt{P} \text{ and } a^*a +b^*b = Q. $$ Then we can define matrices $L$ and $R $ in $M_{(2n,n)}(\bc ) $ by $$ L := \begin{pmatrix}
aP \\bP
\end{pmatrix} \text{ and } R :=  \begin{pmatrix}
aP \\-bP
\end{pmatrix}.$$ It follows that $L^*L = R^*R = P$ and  $ L^*R = X,$ so by Theorem \ref{L*R} we get
\begin{equation} \label{diagP2} 
\|\mathrm{diag}(P)\|_\infty  = \|L\|_c^2 = \|R\|_c^2 =\|L\|_c\|R\|_c \geq \|X\|_S. 
\end{equation}

\smallskip

To prove that there exists a positive $P$ and a self-adjoint contraction $E$ such that $X= \sqrt{P}E\sqrt{P}$ and $\|X\|_S =\|\mathrm{diag}(P)\|_\infty = \|P\|_S,$  we will quote Ando and Okubo \cite{AO} who in their theorem on page 183 state \begin{equation} \label{AOii} 
\|X\|_{S\omega} \leq 1 \iff X = B^*WB, \text{ s. t. } B^*B \circ I \leq I \text{ and } W^*W \leq I.
\end{equation} 
The letter  $\omega $ means that they are working with the numerical radius norm $\|X\|_\omega := \sup |\langle X\xi,\xi\rangle| / \|\xi\|^2,$ but for a self-adjoint matrix this norm is just the operator norm, so for a self-adjoint $ X$  we obtain  a factorization $ X = B^*WB $ with diag$(B^*B) \leq \|X\|_S I_n $ and $\|W\|_\infty \leq 1.$ Then we can define  $P:= B^*B, $ and we   let $V$ denote the partial isometry part of the polar decomposition of $B = V \sqrt{P}.$ Further let  $E:= V^*(Z^* + Z)V/2$ then $X =\sqrt{P}E\sqrt{P}$ with properties as described in the theorem,
 and the proof is complete. 
 \end{proof}

\subsection{Poof of Theorem \ref{cbBSa}} 
\begin{proof}  
 Now let $X$ be a self-adjoint matrix in $M_n(\bc) $ such that $\|X\|_{cbB} = 1,$ and let $X$ have the factorization $X= \Delta(\xi) B \Delta(\xi)$ as described in Theorem \ref{cbBNorm}. We can then define a vector $\l$ in $\bc^n$ with non negative entries and $\sum_j \l_j =1 $  by $\l_j := \xi_j^2.$ The matrix $B$  satisfies $-I_n \leq B \leq I_n$ so  
 \begin{align*} - \Delta(\l) &= -\Delta(\xi)^2 \leq \Delta(\xi)B\Delta(\xi) = X \leq \Delta(\xi)^2 = \Delta(\l) \text{ and } \\ \mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\l)  & =1.\end{align*}  This then implies that
\begin{align} \label{Tr<Bc}
&\forall X= X^*\in M_n(\bc)\,  \exists \Delta(\l) \geq 0:\\ \notag  &- \Delta(\l) \leq X \leq \Delta(\l) \text{ and } \mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\l) ) = \|X\|_{cbB}.
\end{align}

In order to complete the proof we will then show that for any $X=X^* $ in $M_n(\bc) $ and any $\l$ in $\br^n$ with $-\Delta(\l) \leq X \leq \Delta(\l)$ we have that Tr$_n(\Delta(\l)) \geq \|X\|_{cbB}.$ So let such a pair $X$ and $\l$ be given. We first remark that $2\Delta(\l) = (\Delta(\l) - X) + (\Delta(\l) + X) \geq 0.$ so all $\l_j \geq 0.$ Suppose some entries $\l_j $ vanish,  for instance like $\l_j > 0$ for $1 \leq j \leq k$ and $\l_j = 0$ for $k+1 \leq j \leq n, $ and let $E_k$ denote the matrix of the orthogonal projection from $\bc^n $ onto the subspace spanned by the first $k$ canonical  basis vectors $\{\d_1, \dots, \d_k\}$ in $\bc^n.$ Since 
\begin{align*} 0 &\leq  (I_n-E_k)(\Delta(\l) -X)(I_n- E_k )= - (I_n- E_k)X(I_n - E_k ) \\ \text{ and } 0 &\leq  (I_n-E_k)( \Delta(\l))+ X )(I_n- E_k ) =  (I_n- E_k)X(I_n - E_k ) \leq 0 , 
\end{align*} 
we have $( I_n-E_k)(\Delta(\l) +X) (I_n - E_k)= 0  $ and $(\Delta(\l) + X) \geq 0,$ so   $(\Delta(\l) + X)^{(1/2)} (I_n - E_k) = 0,$ and $$ X((I_n- E_k) = (\Delta(\l) + X) (I_n - E_k) = 0,$$ from which it follows that all the columns in X with indices larger than $k$ vanish. Then in the rest of the proof we may, and we will, assume that all $\l_j $ are non  vanishing. This enables us to define a unit vector $\xi$ in $\br^n$ with all $\xi_j >0$ by $\xi_j := \sqrt{\l_j /\mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta( \l)}.$ Then the diagonal matrix $\Delta(\xi)$ is invertible and we can define a self-adjoint  matrix $B$ by $B:= \Delta(\xi)^{-1} X \Delta(\xi)^{-1}$ and $B$ satisfies $$ - \mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\l))I_n  \leq B \leq 
\mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\l))I_n.$$ This implies that  $$\|B\|_\infty \leq \mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\l)) \text{ and } X = \Delta(\xi)B \Delta(\xi),$$ so by item (ii) in Theorem 1.3 of \cite{C4} 
we get  that $\|X\|_{cbB} \leq \mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\l)),$
 whenever $- \Delta(\l) \leq X \leq \Delta(\l)),$ and the theorem follows from \eqref{Tr<Bc} and Theorem 2.1 of \cite{C4}, where the uniqueness of $\xi$ and then also $\l$ is established .  
 \end{proof}

\section{The cases of a general complex  $m \times n$ matrix} \label{GenCase}
The concept of order in matrix  theory  only makes sense for self-adjoint matrices, so it is not immediately possible to extend the theorems \ref{ScSa} and  \ref{cbBSa} to  non self-adjoint matrices, but there is a standard trick to overcome such difficulties. Given a general complex $m \times n $ matrix $X$ we will instead look at the matrix $Y$ in $M_{(m+n)}(\bc) $ given by \begin{equation} \label{Y} Y= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & X\\X^* &0 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation} Fortunately this trick fits well with the  questions we discuss here. We will start with a little lemma.  

\begin{lemma} \label{lemma}
Let $E_m$ in $M_{(m+n)}(\bc)$ be the matrix of the orthogonal projection of $\bc^{(m+n)}$ onto the subspace spanned by the first $m$ canonical basis vectors and let $X$ be in $M_{(m+n)}(\bc),$ then $$\|E_mX(I_{(m+n)} - E_m) + (I_{(m+n)}- E_m)XE_m\|_S \leq \|X\|_S.$$
\end{lemma} 

\begin{proof}  Let $ S $ be the self-adjoint unitary matrix  defined by \newline  $S: =2E_m -I_{(m+n)},$  then the mapping $\Psi$ on $M_{(m+n)}(\bc) $ given by 
$\Psi(X) := E_mX(I_{(m+n)} - E_m) + (I_{(m+n)}- E_m)XE_m$ is given by the formula $\Psi(X) = (1/2)( X - SXS) $ so it is clearly of norm at most 1.
 To estimate $\|\Psi(X)\|_S$ we start with and arbitray $m+n$ matrix $Y$ and recall that the Schur multiplication is denoted $X \circ Y. $ 
We have
$$\|\Psi(X) \circ Y\|_\infty = \|\Psi(X \circ Y) \|_\infty  \leq \|X\circ Y\|_\infty \leq \|X\|_S \|Y\|_\infty,$$
and the lemma follows. 
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition} \label{propo}
Let $X$ be a complex $m \times n$ matrix and $Y$ the self-adjoint matrix  in  $M_{(m+n)}(\bc)$ defined in \eqref{Y} then
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $\|Y\|_S = \|X\|_S$
\item[(ii)] $\|Y\|_{cbB} = 2 \|X\|_{cbB}$
\end{itemize} 
\end{proposition} 

 \begin{proof}
 To prove item (i), it is sufficient to remark that the embedding of $M_{(m,n)}(\bc)$ into the upper right hand  corner of the matrices in $M_{(m+n)}(\bc)$ is isometric, but it may also be given a formal proof following the methods used in the proof of the lemma above.  To show the opposite inequality we remark, that by  Theorem \ref{L*R}  $X = L^*R$ with $L $ in $M_{(r,m)}(\bc),$ $R$ in $M_{(r,n)}(\bc)$ and $\|L\|_c = \|R\|_c = \sqrt{\|X\|_S}.$ Let us then define  a couple of $2r \times (m+n)$  matrices $A$ and $B,$ both with column norm equal to $\sqrt{\|X\|_S}$ by $$ A := \begin{pmatrix}L &0\\0 & R 
\end{pmatrix}  \text{ and } B := \begin{pmatrix}0&R\\L &0\end{pmatrix}.$$  By computation we get that $A^*B = Y,$ so by Theorem \ref{L*R} it follows that $\|Y\|_S \leq \|X\|_S$ and item (i) follows. 

With respect to item (ii) we remind you that the norm $\|.\|_{cbB} $ is dual to the Schur multiplier norm, so for a given $X$ there exists a complex $m \times n $ matrix $W$ such that $\|W\|_S = 1 $ and $ \mathrm{Tr}_n(W^*X) = \|X\|_{cbB},$
 so Tr$_m(WX^*) = \overline{\mathrm{Tr}_m(XW^*)} = \overline{\mathrm{Tr}_n(W^*X)} = \|X\|_{cbB}.$   Let the matrix $Z$ in  $M_{(m+n)}(\bc) $ be given by $$Z:= \begin{pmatrix}
0&W \\W^*& 0
\end{pmatrix},$$ then by item (i) we have $\|Z\|_S = 1$ and by computation we get \newline Tr$_{(m+n)}(ZY) = 2\|X\|_{cbB}, $ so $\|Y\|_{cbB} \geq 2\|X\|_{cbB}.$ 

 To establish the opposite inequality we will - again - use the duality between the Schur multiplier norm and the cbB-norm. Hence there exists a self-adjoint $Z$ in $M_{(m+n)}(\bc) $ such that $\|Z\|_S = 1 $ and Tr$_{(m+n)}(ZY) = \|Y\|_{cbB}.$ 
 We define a matrix $W$ in $M_{(m,n)}(\bc) $ as the $(1,2)$ entry of   $Z$ when this matrix is written as a block matrix, or slightly sloppy $ W = E_m Z(I_{(m+n)}-E_m).$ By item (i) of this proposition and the lemma above  we have that
 \begin{equation} \|W\|_S = \|\Psi(Z)\|_S \leq \|Z\|_S = 1. \end{equation}
 We can then bound the norm $\|Y\|_{cbB}$ upwards as follows \begin{equation}
 \|Y\|_{cbB} = \mathrm{Tr}_{(m+n)}(ZY) \leq  2 | \mathrm{Tr}_n( W^*X)| \leq 2 \|W\|_S\|X\|_{cbB} \leq 2\|X\|_{cbB},
\end{equation}   
and the proposition follows.
 \end{proof}

The proposition just above may now be combined with Theorem \ref{ScSa} to show the following Theorem, but we will omit that proof since the theorem is well known as a result due to Haagerup \cite{UH}. That theorem is presented by Ando and  Okubo \cite{AO} as a consequence of their factorization theorem, and it is the background for exercise 8.8 in \cite{Pa}. 

\begin{theorem} \label{SnormGen}
Let $X$ be a complex $m \times n$ matrix and let $Y$ be the self-adjoint matrix in $M_{(m+n)}(\bc)$ defined in \eqref{Y}  then
\begin{equation} \notag
\|X\|_S = \min \{\|\mathrm{diag}(D)\|_\infty\, : \, D = \begin{pmatrix}
D_1& 0_{(m,n)} \\ 0_{(n,m)}& D_2
\end{pmatrix}, D \geq Y\}.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}


The result for the norm $\|X\|_{cbB}$ has a very short proof.

\begin{theorem} \label{CbBnormGen}
Let $X$ be a complex $m \times n$ matrix and let $Y$ be the self-adjoint matrix in $M_{(m+n)}(\bc)$ defined in  \eqref{Y}, then $$\|X\|_{cbB} = \frac{1}{2}\min\{\mathrm{Tr}_{(m+n)}\big(\Delta(\l)\big)\,: \, \l \in \br^{(m+n)}, \,  \Delta(\l) \geq Y\}.$$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The self-adjoint unitary  $S:= 2E_m - I_{(m+n)} $ has the property that $S\Delta(\l)S = \Delta(\l) $ and $SYS = - Y,$ and this has the effect that if $\Delta(\l) \geq Y$ then  $\Delta(\l) \geq - Y,$  and by Theorem \ref{cbBSa} plus item (ii) of Proposition \ref{propo}  we get
$$\frac{1}{2}\min\{\mathrm{Tr}_{(m+n)}\big(\Delta(\l)\big)\,: \, \l \in \br^{(m+n)},  \Delta(\l) \geq Y\} = \frac{1}{2} \|Y\|_{cbB} = \|X\|_{cbB}$$ and the theorem follows.
\end{proof}




\section{The computation of the  cbB-norm as a linear program} \label{LP} 


The Theorem \ref{cbBSa} is easily reformulated into a {\em linear program,} whose optimal value is the completely bounded bilinear norm of the given  self-adjoint $n \times n $  matrix $X$. This linear
   minimizing  program is not entirely in  the standard form, since the number of linear constraints is infinite. On the other hand the proof of Theorem \ref{cbBSa} shows that the program has an optimal solution.  We will use part of the words used in {\em linear programming theory,} so any vector representing a set  of variables is called a solution, the words {\em feasible solution} means a vector of variables, which satisfies all the linear constraints and an optimal solution is a feasible solution, which solves the linear program.   The dual of this program has  an optimal solution, which only involves a finite number of non vanishing variables, and we will call any solution with only a finite number of non vanishing variables a {\em finite solution.} The optimal finite solution to the dual program, which we produce,  is  found as a consequence of the duality between the 2 norms $\|.\|_{cbB}$ and $\|.\|_S$ and the characterization of the latter norm as a factorization norm, as presented in Theorem \ref{L*R}.

 Let $\cas$ denote the set of unit vectors in $\bc^n,$ then the linear program $(\cp)$ is defined as: 
    \begin{align*} (\cp):  &\min \sum_{j = 1 }^n  \l_j\\  &\text{under the constraints }\\ & \forall j:  \l_j \geq 0\\ & \forall \xi \in \cas: \sum_{j=1}^n  \l_j|\xi_j|^2 \geq \langle X\xi, \xi\rangle\\ & \forall \eta \in \cas: 
   \sum_{j=1}^n  \l_j|\eta_j|^2 \geq -\langle X\eta, \eta\rangle.
   \end{align*}
  The dual program $(P^*)$ becomes:

  \begin{align*}(\cp^*):  &\max  \, \bigg(\sum_{\xi \in \cas} \mu_\xi \langle X\xi, \xi\rangle -  \sum_{\eta \in \cas} \nu_\eta\langle X\eta, \eta \rangle \bigg)\\ 
  &\text{under the constraints }\\ & \forall \xi \in \cas : \mu_\xi \geq 0 , \, \forall  \eta \in \cas: \nu_\eta \geq 0\\ & \forall j \in \{ 1, \dots, n \}: \sum_{\xi \in \cas} \mu_\xi |\xi_j|^2 + \sum_{\eta \in \cas} \nu_\eta |\eta_j|^2 \leq 1.
   \end{align*}

 In the following theorem we will need a special Schur factorization $Z= L^*R$ (Theorem \ref{L*R}), in the case when $Z$ is self-adjoint. The result we will use is Proposition 1.6 in \cite{C4}, and it is copied just below. 

   \begin{proposition} \label{SaSfac}
Let $Z$ be a self-adjoint matrix in $M_n(\bc),$ then there exist  matrices  $E$ and $S$ in $M_n(\bc)$ such that $ E \geq  0, $ $ \mathrm{diag}(E^2) \leq \|Z\|_S I_n,$ $S$ is a self-adjoint isometry and $ Z= ESE.$    
   \end{proposition}

   We will say that such a factorization of $Z$ is a {\em self-adjoit Schur factorization.} 

   We will use the result stated in  the coming lemma a couple of times in the proof of Theorem \ref{LpSol}, and in order to present this lemma we need a definition first. 
   \begin{definition} Let $\xi $ be a unit vector in $\bc^n,$ then $ P_\xi$ denotes the orthogonal projection onto the space $\bc\xi.$
  \end{definition} 

   \begin{lemma} \label{lemFeas} 
    Let  $\big( (\mu_\xi)_{(\xi \in \cas)}, \, (\nu_\eta)_{( \eta \in \cas)} \big)$ be a finite solution to $(P^*)$  and \begin{equation} \label{ZfromSol} Z := \sum_{\xi \in \cas} \mu_\xi P_\xi + \sum_{\eta \in \cas }\nu_\eta P_\eta. \end{equation}
   This finite solution is feasible if and only if $\|Z\|_S \leq 1.$ 
   \end{lemma} 
  \begin{proof}
  For any vector $\g$ in $\cas$ and any $j$ in $\{1, \dots, n\}$ we have $$ |\g_j|^2 = | \langle \g,\d_j \rangle |^2 = \|P_\g \d_j\|^2 = \langle P_\g \d_j, \d_j \rangle = (P_\g)_{(j,j)}.$$
  Let us fix an index $j$ then \begin{equation}
 \sum_{\xi \in \cas} \mu_\xi |\xi(j)|^2 + \sum_{\eta \in \cas }\nu_\eta |\eta(j)|^2 =   \sum_{\xi \in \cas} \mu_\xi (P_\xi)_{(j,j)}  + \sum_{\eta \in \cas }\nu_\eta (P_\eta)_{(j,j)}  = Z_{(j,j)},  
\end{equation} and the lemma follows from Schur's Theorem.   
\end{proof}    
   \begin{theorem} \label{LpSol} 
   The optimal value for the programs $(\cp)$ and $ (\cp^*) $ is $\|X\|_{cbB},$ and an optimal solution to $(\cp)$ may be read out of the unique  cbB-factorization of $X.$ 

    Let  $Y$ be a self-adjoint matrix in $M_n(\bc)$ such that $\|Y\|_S = 1$ and  $\mathrm{Tr}_n(YX) = \|X\|_{cbB}.$ Then an optimal solution for $(\cp^*)$ may be obtained from any self-adjoint Schur decomposition of $Y.$  


Let $\big( (\mu_\xi)_{(\xi \in \cas)}, \, (\nu_\eta)_{( \eta \in \cas)} \big)$ be a finite optimal  solution to $(\cp^*),$ 
and let $Z$ be defined by \ref{ZfromSol} then $\|Z\|_S = 1 $ and $\mathrm{Tr}_n(ZX) = \|X\|_{cbB}.$ 
   \end{theorem}
   \begin{proof}
   It is elementary to see that the program $(\cp)$ consists in finding the infimum $$\inf\{\mathrm{Tr}_n(\Delta(\l))\, : \, -\Delta(\l) \leq X \leq \Delta(\l)\}.$$
   According to Theorem \ref{cbBSa} this infimum is a minimal value equal to $\|X\|_{cbB}, $ which by \eqref{Tr<Bc} is obtained from the decomposition\newline  $X = \Delta(\xi)B\Delta(\xi ) $ such that an  optimal $\l$ is  given by  $\l_j := \|X\|_{cbB}\xi_j^2.$  
   \smallskip



Let us turn to the dual program, and the construction of an optimal solution for $(\cp^*).$  We start by showing, that there exists a finite feasible solution to $(\cp^*),$ such that the value of the object function for this feasible solution is $\|X\|_{cbB}.$ Then let $Y$ be a self-adjoint matrix such that $\|Y\|_S = 1 $ and Tr$_n(YX) = \|X\|_{cbB}.$ By the Proposition \ref{SaSfac} we can write $Y = ESE$ with $E$ positive, diag$(E^2) \leq I_n$ and $S =F-G$ with two orthogonal projections $F $ and $G.$ 

   To the positive matrix $EFE$ there exists an orthonormal set of vectors $\{\xi_1, \dots \xi_k\},$   and strictly positive reals $ \{\mu_1 , \dots, \mu_k\}$ such that $\sum_s \mu_s P_{\xi_s}  = EFE.$ Similarly we can find an orthonormal  set of vectors $\{\eta_1, \dots, \eta_l\},$  and strictly positive reals $\{\nu_1, \dots, \nu_l\}$ such that  $EGE =\sum_t \nu_t P_{\eta_t} .$  

   We will  now define the coefficients $\mu_\xi$ and $\nu_\eta$ of a   solution to  $(\cp^*)$ by $$ \mu_\xi := \begin{cases} \mu_s \text{ if } \xi =\xi_s \\ 0 \,\, \text{ else} \end{cases} \quad  \nu_\eta := \begin{cases} \nu_t \text{ if } \eta  =\eta_t \\ 0 \,\, \text{ else} \end{cases}.$$ Let us show that this constitutes a feasible solution, and to do that we go back to equation \eqref{LpSol} and define $Z$ from this equation, and we see that $Z= EFE + EGE \leq E^2 ,$ so $0 \leq \mathrm{diag}(Z) \leq I_n$ and the  solution is feasible by lemma  \ref{lemFeas}.  Next we  will now compute the value of this feasible solution.
 \begin{align}
 &  \sum_{\xi \in \cas} \mu_\xi \langle X\xi, \xi\rangle -  \sum_{\eta \in \cas} \nu_\eta\langle X\eta, \eta \rangle = \sum_{s=1}^k \mu_s\langle X\xi_s, \xi_s\rangle - \sum_{t = 1}^l \nu_t \langle X \eta_t, \eta_t \rangle \\ \notag =& \sum_{s=1}^k \mu_s\mathrm{Tr}_n( XP_{\xi_s} )  - \sum_{t = 1}^l \nu_t \mathrm{Tr}_n( X P_{\eta_t}) =    \mathrm{Tr}_n(X(EFE - EGE ) )\\ \notag =&  \mathrm{Tr}_n(XY)  =   \|X\|_{cbB},
\end{align}   
and the optimal value for $(\cp^*)$ is at least $\|X\|_{cbB}.$ 


 Let  $\{(\xi_1, \mu_1), \dots (\xi_k, \mu_k)\} $ and $ \{(\eta_1, \nu_1), \dots, (\eta_l,\nu_l)\}$  be a finite  feasible solution, then we will now  show, that its value is at most  $\|X\|_{cbB}.$  We will define  positive matrices $A$ and $B$ by $$ A:= \sum_{s=1}^k \mu_s P_{\xi_s} , \quad B:= \sum_{t=1}^l \nu_t P_{\eta_t} ,$$ then by Lemma \ref{lemFeas} the feasibility of the solution shows that $\|A + B\|_S \leq 1.$  
We can then define a self-adjoint matrix $Z: = A - B$ and and a positive matrix $P:= A+B$ then $$ - P = -B-A \leq A- B = Z \leq A+B = P$$ so by Theorem \ref{ScSa}  we have $\|Z\|_S \leq \|P\|_S,$  and by the feasibility and Lemma  \ref{lemFeas} we have $\|Z\|_S\leq 1.$ 
Let us now bound  the value of our feasible solution 
   \begin{align}
 &  \sum_{\xi \in \cas} \mu_\xi \langle X\xi, \xi\rangle -  \sum_{\eta \in \cas} \nu_\eta\langle X\eta, \eta \rangle = \sum_{s=1}^k \mu_s\langle X\xi_s, \xi_s\rangle - \sum_{t = 1}^l \nu_t \langle X \eta_t, \eta_t \rangle \\ \notag =& \sum_{s=1}^k \mu_s\mathrm{Tr}_n( XP_{\xi_s} )  - \sum_{t = 1}^l \nu_t \mathrm{Tr}_n( X P_{\eta_t}) =    \mathrm{Tr}_n(X(A - B) ) = \mathrm{Tr}_n(XZ) \\ 
 \notag \leq & \|Z\|_S\|X\|_{cbB} \leq \|X\|_{cbB},
\end{align}  
and the theorem follows.  
   \end{proof}


The proof just given contains a proof of the following corollary, which also - by the duality between the norms $\|.\|_S$ and $\|.\|_{cbB}$  -  is a direct consequence of Corollary \ref{ScCor}. 

\begin{corollary}
Let $X$ be a self-adjoint complex $n \times n$ matrix, then $$\|X\|_{cbB} = \max\{ \mathrm{Tr}_n(XY)\, :\, Y=  A-B, A \geq 0, \, B \geq  0, \, \|A+B\|_S \leq 1\}.$$ 
\end{corollary}

