The output SNR plays an important role in non-stationary signal detection performance improvement. By inheriting the definition of the output SNR of the WD \cite{RN117}, the output SNR of the GLWD can be defined as
\begin{align}
\label{(18)}
\text{OSNR}_\text{GLWD} = \nonumber \notag\\
& \hspace{-2.5cm} \frac{\max_{(t,u) \in \mathbb{R}^2} \left| W_{f}^{\mathbf{B}_{1}, \mathbf{B}_{2} , \mathbf{A}_{1}, \mathbf{A}_{2}}(t,u) \right|}
{\underset{\text{argmax}_{(t,u)}}{\textbf{Mean}} \left| W_{f}^{\mathbf{B}_{1}, \mathbf{B}_{2} , \mathbf{A}_{1}, \mathbf{A}_{2}}(t,u) \right| 
\left\{ \textbf{E} \left[ W_{n}^{\mathbf{B}_{1}, \mathbf{B}_{2} , \mathbf{A}_{1}, \mathbf{A}_{2}}(t,u) \right] \right\}} 
% \tag{12} \label{(12)}
\end{align}
where "Mean" denotes the arithmetic mean for a countable set while the integral average for an uncountable one. 

Assuming that \( D \) is the power spectral density of white noise, we obtain the following result.
