    $\text{(i)}\implies \text{(ii)}.$ Assume $\mu(\{H\})=0$. Set $C_c(G)_H=\{f\in C_c(G) : {\rm supp}\,f \subseteq H\}$. Note that $C_c(G)_H \ne \{0\}$ since $H$ is open. Then the formula $$\int_G f(x)\rho(x)\, d\mu_G(x) = \mu(\{H\})\int_H f(x)\, d\mu_H(x)\,\,(f\in C_c(G)_H)$$ implies $\int_G f(x)\rho(x)\, d\mu_G(x) = 0$ for all $f\in C_c(G)_H$. Moreover, since $f/\rho \in C_c(G)_H$, we obtain $\int_G f(x)\, d\mu_G(x) = 0$ for all $f\in C_c(G)_H$. By the inner regularity of $\mu_G$, there exists a compact set $K \subseteq G$ such that $K \subseteq H$ and $0<\mu_G(K)\le \mu_G(H)$. Then, by Urysohn's lemma, there exists a $f\in C_c(G)_H$ which satisfies $0\le f\le 1$ and $f=1$ on $K$. It follows that $0<\int_G f\,d\mu_G $, which is a contradiction.
