\section{Introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Quantum Field Theory (QFT) in curved spacetime \cite{DEWITT1975,Wald:1975kc, Fulling:1989nb, BirrellDavies1982, Wald1994, Mukhanov2007, Witten22QFT} has profoundly reshaped our understanding of quantum phenomena in non-inertial frames and gravitational backgrounds. A cornerstone of this field is the realization that the vacuum state is not unique but observer-dependent, a fact established by seminal results like the Unruh effect \cite{Unruh1976} and Hawking radiation \cite{Hawking1975}. These concepts have far-reaching implications, influencing fields from relativistic quantum information \cite{Landulfo2009, MannRalph2012} to quantum optics \cite{Scully2003, Scully2006, Svidzinsky21prl, Svidzinsky21prr}.

Recent work has established a broad framework for exploring this observer-dependence through families of ``kappa'' ($\kappa$) vacua. The investigation began with the introduction of \textit{$\kappa$-Rindler modes}, which interpolate between the Rindler ($\kappa \to 0$) and Minkowski ($\kappa=1$) vacua \cite{Azizi2022kappashort, Azizi2023}. The physical reality of these states was established in \cite{Azizi2025hottercolderUnruh}, which showed that an accelerated detector interacting with this vacuum perceives a perfect thermal bath with a tunable temperature $T_\kappa = \kappa T_U$. This finding demonstrated that vacua could be engineered to appear ``hotter'' or ``colder'' than the standard Unruh temperature.

Complementing this, the family of \textit{$\kappa$-plane wave modes} was introduced in \cite{Azizi2025KappaPW}. The key insight of that work was that the associated $\kappa$-vacuum is a continuous-mode squeezed state, but one described by a purely \textbf{real} squeezing parameter.

This paper builds directly on that foundation by asking a natural question: what new physics emerges if the squeezing is allowed to be complex? We answer this by introducing a second parameter, $\gamma$, into the $\kappa$-plane wave construction. The resulting \textit{$\kappa\gamma$ plane wave modes} define a two-parameter family of vacua, $\ket{0_{\kappa,\gamma}}$, that reveals a richer vacuum structure and novel physical phenomena. The relationships between these different mode families and their key features are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:kappa_modes_summary}.

We present three central results. First, we uncover the phenomenon of phase-induced particle creation: a mere shift in the phase parameter $\gamma$ induces a non-trivial Bogoliubov transformation, meaning an observer in one $\gamma$-frame will detect particles in the vacuum of another. Second, we demonstrate that the $\ket{0_{\kappa,\gamma}}$ vacuum is a continuous-mode phase-squeezed state—a foundational concept in quantum optics \cite{ScullyZubairy1997} and continuous-variable quantum information \cite{Braunstein2005, Weedbrook2012}—defined by the condition:
\begin{equation}
\left( a_\nu - e^{-\frac{\pi \nu}{\kappa}} e^{2i\gamma} a^\dagger_\nu \right) \ket{0_{\kappa,\gamma}} = 0.
\end{equation}
This generalizes the real squeezing of the $\kappa$-plane wave formalism, with $\kappa$ controlling the squeezing magnitude and $\gamma$ setting its phase. Finally, we derive the generalized Bogoliubov transformations connecting the $\kappa\gamma$ basis to the $\kappa$-Rindler basis, providing a master framework that unifies all standard mode decompositions in this context.

