\section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclusion}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In this work, we have generalized the concept of $\kappa$-deformed field modes by introducing the $\kappa\gamma$ plane wave modes, which depend on both a deformation parameter $\kappa$ and a phase $\gamma$. This extension yields a richer vacuum structure with several novel physical implications.

Our most significant finding is the phenomenon of phase-induced particle creation. We demonstrated that a relative phase shift between two observers' frames of reference is sufficient to cause a Bogoliubov transformation, leading one observer to detect particles in the other's vacuum. The particle number density, given by ${N}_\Lambda = \sin^2(\gamma'-\gamma)/\sinh^2(\pi\Lambda/\kappa)$, makes the physical role of the phase manifest.

Furthermore, we have shown that the $\kappa\gamma$ vacuum, $\ket{0_{\kappa,\gamma}}$, is a continuous-mode phase-squeezed state of the Minkowski vacuum. This characterization of the $\kappa\gamma$-vacuum as a phase-squeezed state is the plane-wave counterpart to the study of the $\kappa$-Rindler vacuum's tunable thermal properties presented in \cite{Azizi2025hottercolderUnruh}. Together, these results provide a comprehensive picture of the $\kappa$-deformed vacuum landscape, viewed through the complementary lenses of squeezing and thermality.

Finally, we derived the generalized ``Mother of All Bogoliubov Transformations'' that include the $\gamma$ parameter, providing a unified mathematical structure that contains all previously known mode decompositions in this context as limiting cases. These results open new avenues for investigating vacuum entanglement, detector physics, and analog gravity models, where the phase parameter $\gamma$ can be explored as a new and physically consequential degree of freedom.


