\begin{proof}
	The existence theorem was proven by \cite{Serre_Albanese}. By \cite{Grothendieck_Picard_et_Albanese} (see also \cite[Remark 2.3]{Achter_Casalaina-Martir_Vial_A_complete_answer_to_Albanese_base_change_for_incomplete_varieties}), we know how $\Alb(X)$ is precisely constructed: since $\Pic^0_{X/k}$ is a proper $k$--group scheme, we know that its reduction is an abelian variety. We then define $\Alb(X)$ to be the dual of this abelian variety. Note that $\Pic^0_{\Alb(X)/k} = (\Pic^0_{X/k})_{\red}$, and it follows from the construction that $\alb_X^* \colon \Pic^0_{\Alb(X)/k} \to \Pic^0_{X/k}$ corresponds to the inclusion $(\Pic^0_{X/k})_{\red} \inc \Pic^0_{X/k}$. In particular, it is injective and we have proven \autoref{inj_Pic}. Since the tangent space at $\cO_X$ of $\Pic^0_{X/k}$ is exactly $H^1(X, \cO_X)$, and similarly for $\Alb(X)$ instead of $X$ (see for example \cite[Theorem 9.5.11]{Kleiman_The_Picard_Scheme}), we obtain \autoref{inj_H1} by construction. The last statement is well--known, see e.g. \cite[Lemma 1.6]{Roessler_Schroer_Varieties_with_free_tangent_sheaves}.
