Let us introduce one more tool, namely the notion of \emph{unit} Cartier modules and $V$--modules. We start with abstract considerations. 

Let $f \colon X \to Y$ be a finite morphism of varieties. It is well-known that the functor $f_*$ has a left adjoint, namely the pullback $f^*$. A perhaps less known fact is that is also has a right adjoint $f^{\flat}$, which lies at the heart of Grothendieck duality theory. Affine locally (say $X = \Spec R$ and $Y = \Spec S$), then it is given by assigning to an $S$--module $N$ the $R$--module \[ f^{\flat}(N) = \Hom_S(R, N). \] 

Note in particular that if $f$ is flat, then $f^{\flat}\cN \cong f^*\cN \otimes f^{\flat}\cO_Y$.

\begin{lem}\label{funny_adjunction_K_trivial}
	Let $\phi \colon A \to B$ be an isogeny of abelian varieties. Then $\phi^* \cong \phi^{\flat}$. In particular, $\phi_*$ is also the left adjoint of $\phi^*$.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
	As already remarked above, we know that $\phi^{\flat}\cM \cong \phi^*\cM \otimes \phi^{\flat}\cO_B$. Since \[ \phi^{\flat}(\cO_B) \cong \phi^{\flat}\omega_B \expl{\cong}{\cite[Exercise III.7.2]{Hartshorne_Algebraic_Geometry}} \omega_A \cong \cO_A, \] where the extremal isomorphisms come from the fact that $\omega_B \cong \cO_B$ and $\omega_A \cong \cO_A$, since $A$ and $B$ are abelian varieties. We then conclude that $\phi^{\flat} \cong \phi^*$.
\end{proof}

In particular, if $\theta \colon \cN \to V^*\cN$ is a morphism, then by \autoref{funny_adjunction_K_trivial} we automatically obtain an adjoint morphism $V_*\cN \to \cN$.

\begin{defn}
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item We say that a Cartier module $F_*\cM \to \cM$ on a variety $X$ is \emph{unit} if the adjoint structural morphism $\cM \to F^{\flat}\cM$ is an isomorphism. 
		\item We say that a $V$--module $\cN \to V^*\cN$ on an abelian variety is \emph{unit} if its adjoint structural morphism $V_*\cN \to \cN$ is unit.
	\end{enumerate}
\end{defn}

\begin{example}
	A local computation shows that if $X$ is a smooth projective variety, then the canonical Cartier module structure on $\omega_X$ gives a unit Cartier module. In fact, this is also true whenever $X$ is normal.

	Analogously, the standard $V$--module structure on the skyscraper sheaf $k(0)$ on an abelian variety is unit.
\end{example}

Unit Cartier modules carry enjoyable properties; perhaps one of the most important is that treating it as a Cartier crystal or as a Cartier module is somewhat equivalent (see e.g. \cite[Corollary 3.4.15]{Baudin_Duality_between_perverse_sheaves_and_Cartier_crystals}). Here is an instance of that philosophy:

\begin{lem}\label{super_basic_unit_Cartier_module}
	Let $\cM$ be a unit Cartier module on a variety $X$, and let $\cN \inc \cM$ be a non--zero sub--Cartier module. Then $\cN$ is not nilpotent.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
	Let $e > 0$. Since $\cN \inj \cM$ is a sub--Cartier module, there exists by definition a diagram \[ \begin{tikzcd}
		F^s_*\cN \arrow[rr] \arrow[d, hook] &  & \cN \arrow[d, hook] \\
		F^s_*\cM \arrow[rr]                 &  & \cM.               
	\end{tikzcd} \] By adjunction, we also obtain a diagram \[ \begin{tikzcd}
		\cN \arrow[rr] \arrow[d, hook] &  & {F^{s, \flat}\cN} \arrow[d, hook] \\
		\cM \arrow[rr]                 &  & {F^{s, \flat}\cM.}                
	\end{tikzcd} \] Given that $\cM \to F^{es, \flat}\cM$ is in particular injective, so must be $\cN \to F^{es, \flat}\cN$. We then conclude by adjunction that $F^{es}_*\cN \to \cN$ cannot be zero. Since $s > 0$ was arbitrary, we deduce that $\cN$ is not nilpotent.
\end{proof}

We finish these preparations with a characterization of the $p$--rank of an abelian variety in terms existence of certain $V$--modules. Before proving it, let us remark that since $V^* \cong V^{\flat}$ (see \autoref{funny_adjunction_K_trivial}), then the datum of a morphism $\cN \to V^{s, *}\cN$ is \emph{equivalent} to the datum of a morphism $\cN \to V^{s, \flat}\cN$. Seeing it this way has a serious advantage: if $f \colon B \inj A$ is a finite morphism of abelian subvarieties and $\cN$ is a $V$--module on $A$, then $f^{\flat}\cN$ is naturally a $V$--module on $B$. Similarly, if $\cN'$ is a $V$--module on $B$, then $f_*\cN'$ is a $V$--module on $A$. The natural adjunction between $f_*$ and $f^{\flat}$ on coherent sheaves therefore extends to an adjunction at the level of $V$--modules.

\begin{rem}
	There is surely a functor $f^*$ on $V$--modules between abelian varieties, but it is \emph{not} a left adjoint of $f_*$ in general! 

	Let us illustrate this. Note that $\cO_{\bighat{A}}$ is naturally a $V$--module. Let $i_0 \colon \Spec k(0) \to \bighat{A}$ denote the inclusion of $0 \in \bighat{A}$. If there existed a non--zero morphism of $V$--modules $\cO_{\bighat{A}} \to i_{0, *}i_0^*\cO_{\bighat{A}} = k(0)$, then applying $\FM_A$ and \autoref{properties_Fourier_Mukai_transform} would give us a non--zero morphism $\omega_A \to k(0)$ of Cartier modules. This morphism cannot be injective as long as $\dim(A) > 0$, so it must be zero since $\omega_A$ is simple as a Cartier module (see \autoref{regular_implis_simple}).

	The issue lies in the fact that the isomorphism $V^* \cong V^{\flat}$ (which explains why we can take pushforwards of $V$--modules) is not natural in the abelian variety $A$, as it depends on a choice of a nowhere vanishing top--form on $A$.
\end{rem}

As a consequence of this construction, we have the following structural result on $V$--modules supported at a single point.

\begin{lem}\label{filtration_for_V_modules_supp_at_0}
	Let $\cN$ be a $V$--module on $\bighat{A}$ supported at $0$. Then there is a filtration $0 = \cN_0 \inc \cN_1 \inc \dots \inc \cN_n = \cN$ of $V$--modules, such that for all $0 \leq i \leq n - 1$, $\cN_{i + 1}/\cN_i \cong k(0)$, where $k(0)$ is either endowed with the zero $V$--module structure, or with its canonical $V$--module structure (coming from the Cartier structure of $\omega_A$ via $\FM_A$).
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
	Let $i_0 \colon \Spec k(0) \to \bighat{A}$ denote the natural map. In order to avoid confusions, for this argument only, we shall write $i_{0, *}k(0)$ to denote the module on $\bighat{A}$, while $k(0)$ stands for the module on the field $k(0) = k$.

	First, note that since $V = F^{-1}$ on $k(0)$, we obtain that $V^* \cong F_*$. In other words, a $V$--module structure on a $k(0)$--vector space $M$ is the same as a morphism $M \to F^s_*M$, i.e. a $p^s$--linear endomorphism of $M$. Such an object is usually called a Frobenius module, and an important structural theorem says that any Frobenius module $M$ on $k(0)$ is isomorphic to a direct sum of the form $(k(0), (\cdot)^{p^s})^{\oplus j} \oplus (M', \phi)$ where $\phi$ is nilpotent (see e.g. \cite[Corollary p.143]{Mumford_Abelian_Varieties}). In particular, any Frobenius module (i.e. $V$--module) has a filtration with subquotients isomorphic to $(k(0), \psi)$ where $\psi$ is either the canonical Frobenius module structure, or zero.

	Let us now show the result by induction on the length of $\cN$. We have an exact sequence \[ 0 \to i_{0, *}i_0^{\flat}\cN \to \cN \to \cC \to 0, \] where $\length(\cC) < \length(\cN)$. The $V$--module $i_{0, *}i_0^{\flat}\cN$ is the pushforward of a $V$--module on $k(0)$. Since $i_{0, *}i_0^{\flat}\cN$ has a filtration of the required form by the paragraph above and $\length(\cC) < \length(\cN)$, we conclude the proof by induction.
\end{proof}

As a consequence, we obtain the following characterization of ordinary abelian varieties.

\begin{cor}\label{characterization_ordinary_varieties}
	Let $r(A)$ denote the $p$--rank of $A$. For any integer $0 \leq j \leq g$, the following are equivalent:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item\label{basic_facts_AV_itm:there_exists} there exists a $V$--module $\cN$ such that $\Supp(\cN) = \{0\}$ and \[ \colim \Tor_j(V^{es, *} \cN,  k(0)) \neq 0; \] 
			\item\label{basic_facts_AV_itm:for_all} for any non--nilpotent $V$--module $\cN$ such that $\Supp(\cN) = \{0\}$, we have \[ \colim \Tor_j(V^{es, *} \cN,  k(0)) \neq 0; \] 
			\item\label{basic_facts_AV_itm:p_rank} $r(A) \geq g - j$.
		\end{enumerate}
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
	Let us now show that \autoref{basic_facts_AV_itm:p_rank} $\implies$ \autoref{basic_facts_AV_itm:for_all}, so assume that $r(A) \geq g - j$. As in \autoref{first_example}, we have that \[ \colim \Tor_j(V^{es, *}k(0), k(0)) \neq 0, \] where $k(0)$ is given its canonical $V$--module structure. Hence, we conclude by \autoref{filtration_for_V_modules_supp_at_0} and long exact sequences in homology.

	We are left to show that \autoref{basic_facts_AV_itm:there_exists} $\implies$ \autoref{basic_facts_AV_itm:p_rank}. Consider a filtration as in \autoref{filtration_for_V_modules_supp_at_0}. Again by the long exact sequence in homology, we obtain that \[ \colim \Tor_j(V^{es, *}k(0), k(0)) \neq 0, \] where the $V$--structure on $k(0)$ corresponds to the canonical Cartier module structure on $\omega_A$. We are then done by the computations in \autoref{first_example}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Proof of the birational characterization}

Throughout, fix a normal proper variety $X$ such that $\kappa(X, \omega_X) = 0$, $H^0_{ss}(X, \omega_X) \neq 0$ and $b_1(X) = 2\dim(X)$. To lighten notations, we denote by $a \colon X \to A$ the Albanese morphism of $X$. We also let $\cN_{\injj}$ denote the canonical injective $V$--module associated to $a_*\omega_X$. \\

Our proof will follow similar ideas as in \cite{Hacon_Patakfalvi_Zhang_Bir_char_of_AVs}, with a few adjustments and shortcuts. The plan is to show that $a_*\omega_X$ has rank one. The main steps are the following:

\begin{enumerate}
	\item Show that $\Supp(\cN_{\injj}) = V^0_{\injj}(a_*\omega_X) = \{0_{\bighat{A}}\}$, and deduce in particular surjectivity of $a$ and ordinarity of $A$;
	\item The previous step shows that $\cN_{\injj}$ has finite length. By performing a base change along the Verschiebung, we show that $\cN_{\injj}$ must be of length one. Equivalently, $a_*\omega_X$ has rank one ``up to nilpotence''.
	\item Conclude the proof.
\end{enumerate}


\begin{lem}\label{very_basic_trick}
	For any line bundle $L$ on $X$ which is torsion but not trivial, we have that $H^0(X, \omega_X \otimes L) = 0$.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
	Assume that there exists a divisor $D$ on $X$ such that $nD \sim 0$, and such that there exists an effective divisor $E \sim K_X + D$. We will show that $D \sim 0$. Since $h^0(X, \omega_X) > 0$ by assumption, there exists an effective divisor $E' \sim K_X$. But then, $nE' \sim nK_X \sim nE$. Since $h^0(X, \cO_X(nK_X)) = 1$ and both $E$ and $E'$ are effective, we obtain an \emph{equality} of divisors $nE' = nE$. In particular, $E' = E$, so $K_X \sim K_X + D$. In other words, $D \sim 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lem}
	We have that $V^0_{\injj}(a_*\omega_X) = \{0\}$. In other words, $\cN_{\injj}$ is supported at $0$.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
	Since $0 \neq H^0_{ss}(X, \omega_X) = H^0_{ss}(A, a_*\omega_X)$, we obtain that \[ 0 \in W^0_F(a_*\omega_X) \expl{\inc}{\autoref{generic vanishing}.\autoref{van_and_non_van}} \bigcup_{e > 0}(p^e)^{-1}W^0(a_*\omega_X) \expl{\inc}{by construction} \bigcup_{e > 0}(p^e)^{-1}V^0_{\injj}(a_*\omega_X). \] Hence, $0 \in V^0_{\injj}(a_*\omega_X)$. 

	To see the converse inclusion, note that if $\alpha$ is a torsion point in $V^0_{\injj}(a_*\omega_X) \inc V^0(a_*\omega_X)$, then $H^0(X, \omega_X \otimes a^*\cP_{-\alpha}) \neq 0$. If $\alpha \neq 0$, then $\cP_{\-\alpha} \not\cong \cO_A$, so $a^*\cP_{-\alpha} \not\cong \cO_X$ by \autoref{properties_Albanese}.\autoref{inj_Pic}. Thus, we obtain by \autoref{very_basic_trick} that $V^0_{\injj}(a_*\omega_X)$ does not contain any torsion point aside from $0$. Since torsion points are dense in 
	$V^0_{\injj}(a_*\omega_X)$ by \cite[Proposition 6.1]{Pink_Roessler_Manin_Mumford_conjecture} (with their terminology, $\bighat{A}$ is positive since the map $p^{es}$ is never an isomorphism on a non--trivial abelian variety), the proof is complete.
\end{proof}

\begin{cor}\label{alb_surj_and_ordinary}
	The Albanese morphism $a \colon X \to A$ is surjective, generically finite, and $A$ is ordinary.
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
	We will show that $a_*\omega_X$ is supported everywhere on $A$ (this automatically shows that $a$ is surjective). By construction, there is a nil--isomorphism $\cH^0\FM_A(a_*\omega_X) \surj \cN_{\injj}$. Applying the symmetric Fourier--Mukai transform again and \autoref{equivalence_V_crystals_and_Cartier_crystals} shows that $a_*\omega_X \sim_C \cH^0\FM_A(\cN_{\injj})$. Note that $\cV \coloneqq \cH^0\FM_A(\cN_{\injj})$ is a unipotent vector bundle by \autoref{properties_Fourier_Mukai_transform}.\autoref{itm:equiv_cat_unipotent}, and its Cartier module structure $\theta \colon F^s_*\cV \to \cV$ is not nilpotent (otherwise $\cN_{\injj} = 0$, contradicting \autoref{very_basic_trick}). If $a_*\omega_X$ was supported on a closed subset $Z$ with complement $U$, then surely \[ 0 = a_*\omega_X|_U \sim_C \cV|_U. \] However, $\cV$ is torsion--free, so given that its Cartier module structure is not nilpotent, it cannot become nilpotent when restricting to some non--empty open subset. Thus, $a_*\omega_X$ is indeed supported everywhere and $a$ is surjective.

	Since $b_1(X) = 2\dim X$, we conclude that $\dim(X) = \dim(A)$ by \autoref{properties_Albanese}.\autoref{dim_Alb}, so $a$ is automatically generically finite. The fact that $A$ is ordinary follows from \autoref{characterization_ordinary_varieties}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lem}\label{lucky_injection_right_way}
	There is a canonical injection $\phi \colon \cV \inj a_*\omega_X$ of Cartier modules, where $\cV$ is a unipotent vector bundle on $A$ or rank equal to $\length(\cN_{\injj})$. Furthermore, $\phi$ is a nil--isomorphism.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
	First, note that for any $\cO_{\bighat{A}, 0}$--module $N$ of finite length, then $\length(V^{s, *}N) = \length(N)$ (we see $V^s$ as a morphism $\cO_{\bighat{A}, 0} \to \cO_{\bighat{A}, 0})$. Indeed, since $V \colon \cO_{\bighat{A}, 0} \to \cO_{\bighat{A}, 0}$ is flat, it preserves exact sequences, so it is enough to show that $V^*$ preserves the residue field. This is equivalent to saying that $V$ is unramified, so we are done by \autoref{basic facts about AV} since $A$ is ordinary (see \autoref{alb_surj_and_ordinary}).

	Given that the structural morphism $\cN_{\injj} \to V^{s, *}\cN_{\injj}$ is injective by assumption, it must be an isomorphism on the stalk at $0$ by the above paragraph. In particular, any $V$--module which is a subquotient of $\cN_{\injj}$ satisfies the same property, so any non--zero subquotient of $\cN_{\injj}$ cannot be nilpotent. Since the cokernel of the adjoint structural map $V^s_*\cN_{\injj} \to \cN_{\injj}$ is nilpotent by definition, we deduce that this map is surjective. Since both $V^s_*\cN_{\injj}$ and $\cN_{\injj}$ have the same length (this does not depend on the fact that $V$ is étale), we deduce that $\cN_{\injj}$ is a unit $V$--module.

	In particular, the Cartier module $\cV \coloneqq \FM_A(\cN_{\injj})$ is unit by \autoref{properties_Fourier_Mukai_transform}.\autoref{itm:behaviour_pushforwards_and_pullbacks}. Note that it is a unipotent vector bundle of the sought rank by \autoref{properties_Fourier_Mukai_transform}.\autoref{itm:equiv_cat_unipotent}. Applying $\FM_{\bighat{A}}$ to the non--zero composition \[ \FM_A(a_*\omega_A) \to \cH^0(\FM_A(a_*\omega_X)) \to \cN_{\injj}, \] gives in particular a non--zero morphism $\phi \colon \cV \to a_*\omega_X$ of Cartier modules. We are left to show that $\phi$ is injective.

	Since the surjection of $V$--modules $\cH^0(\FM_A(a_*\omega_X)) \to \cN_{\injj}$ is a nil--isomorphism by definition, we obtain by \autoref{equivalence_V_crystals_and_Cartier_crystals} that $\phi$ is a nil--isomorphism. In particular, $\ker(\phi)$ is nilpotent. Given that $\cV$ is a unit Cartier module, we conclude that $\ker(\phi) = 0$ by \autoref{super_basic_unit_Cartier_module}.
\end{proof}

\begin{prop}\label{alb_purely_insep}
	We have that $\length(\cN_{\injj}) = 1$. In particular, $\cV = \omega_A$ and the morphism $\phi \colon \omega_A \inj a_*\omega_X$ from \autoref{lucky_injection_right_way} is a nil--isomorphism.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
	Let $\fm$ denote the maximal ideal of $\cO_{\bighat{A}, 0}$. Since the coherent sheaf $\cN_{\injj}$ is supported at $0_{\bighat{A}}$, it must be annihilated by $\fm^e$ for $e \gg 0$. In particular, $F^e_*\cN_{\injj}$ is annihilated by $\fm$ for $e \gg 0$, so it is a $k(0)$--vector space. It must then be free, so there exists an isomorphism $F^e_*\cN_{\injj} \cong k(0)^{\oplus j}$, where $j \coloneqq \length(\cN_{\injj})$. Applying $\FM_{\bighat{A}}$ and \autoref{properties_Fourier_Mukai_transform} gives us that $V^{e, *}(\cV) \cong \cO_A^{\oplus j}$. Consider the base change diagram \[
	\begin{tikzcd}
		Y \arrow[r, "\psi"] \arrow[d, "b"'] & X \arrow[d, "a"]              \\
		A \arrow[r, "V^e"']                     & A.               
	\end{tikzcd} 
	\] Since we have an injection $\cV \inj a_*\omega_X$ by \autoref{lucky_injection_right_way}, we also obtain an injection \[ \cO_A^{\oplus j} \cong V^{e, *}(\cV) \inj V^{e, *}a_*\omega_X \expl{\cong}{flat base change} b_*\psi^*\omega_X. \] In particular, $h^0(Y, \psi^*\omega_X) \geq j$. If we could show that $Y$ was connected (and hence normal since $\psi$ is étale and $X$ is normal), then we would deduce that \[ \kappa(Y, \psi^*\omega_X) = \kappa(X, \omega_X) = 0, \] so this would force $j = 1$ and the proof would be complete.

	Hence, all we need to show is that $h^0(Y, \cO_Y) = 1$, or equivalently that $h^0(X, \psi_*\cO_Y) = 1$. By flat base change, this is the same as showing that $h^0(X, a^*(V^{e}_*\cO_A)) = 1$. Since $\FM_A(V^{e}_*\cO_A) \cong F^{e, *}k(0)$ is supported at $0$, we deduce that $V^e_*\cO_A$ is a unipotent vector bundle (use \autoref{properties_Fourier_Mukai_transform}) that satisfies $h^0(A, V^e_*\cO_A) = h^0(A, \cO_A) = 1$. Hence, we are done by \autoref{albanese_preserves_indec_vbs}.
\end{proof}

\begin{cor}[{\autoref{non_eff_characterization}}]\label{final_cor}
	We have that $\rank(a_*\omega_X) = 1$. In particular, the Albanese morphism $a \colon X \to A$ is birational.
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
	Since $\phi$ is a nil--isomorphism by \autoref{alb_purely_insep}, there exists $e > 0$ such that the image of $F^{es}_*(a_*\omega_X) \to a_*\omega_X$ factors through $\cV$. On the other hand, the trace map on $\omega_X$ is generically surjective (it is surjective on the regular locus by \autoref{regular_implis_simple}). Since $a$ is generically finite (\autoref{alb_surj_and_ordinary}), we obtain that $F^s_*(a_*\omega_X) \to a_*\omega_X$ is generically surjective too, so the injection $\cV \inj a_*\omega_X$ is generically an isomorphism. Given that $0 \neq \rank(\cM) \leq \rank(\omega_A) = 1$, we conclude that $\rank(a_*\omega_X) = 1$. 

	Since $a$ is generically finite, we know that $1 = \rank(a_*\omega_X) = \deg(a)\rank(\omega_X)$, so $1 = \deg(a)$. In other words, $a$ is birational.
\end{proof}

\begin{rem}
	One can generalize the above proof in several ways, in increasing order of difficulty:

	\begin{itemize}
		\item Relax the hypothesis that $H^0_{ss}(X, \omega_X) \neq 0$ (and hence $\Alb(X)$ might not be ordinary anymore). The proof of \autoref{lucky_injection_right_way} nevertheless gives the existence of a morphism from a (potentially non--unit) unipotent vector bundle to $a_*\omega_X$. Using a trick similar to what is done in the proof of \autoref{final_cor} gives that this morphism is generically surjective. It turns out that this is enough for the proof. The actual price that one has to pay is that the cover trivializing $\cV$ might not be étale anymore, so this introduces worse singularities on the étale cover of $X$. Nevertheless, one can work their way through this issue. This is the approach taken in \cite{Hacon_Patakfalvi_Zhang_Bir_char_of_AVs}.

		An other way to solve this issue is to completely stay in the realm of Cartier crystals, and obtain via the proof of \autoref{lucky_injection_right_way} the existence of an isomorphism of crystals $a_*\omega_X \sim_C \cV'$, where $\cV'$ is a unit, unipotent vector bundle (it is a quotient of $\cV$). Then we only need an étale base change to trivialize $\cV'$, but we cannot compare global sections of the pullback of $\cV'$ and those of the pullback of $a_*\omega_X$, since we only have an isomorphism at the level of crystals (there is no explicit map of coherent sheaves between $\cV'$ and $a_*\omega_X$ giving rise to this isomorphism of crystals). Furthermore, we cannot compare semi--stable global sections (which is invariant under isomorphisms of crystals), since both groups will be zero if $A$ is not ordinary. The trick is to compare the top semi--stable cohomology group, which is insensitive to ordinarity. At the end though, we can only deduce from this approach that the finite part of $a$ is purely inseparable, not an isomorphism. Separability has to be dealt differently (note that the proof of \cite{Hacon_Patakfalvi_Zhang_Bir_char_of_AVs} starts by showing separability in order to be able to deal with the singularities that they create). The advantage of this approach is that it is useful for the next generalizations below.

		\item Relax the hypothesis $b_1(X) = 2\dim(X)$, and even working including boundaries in the picture. A similar proof as in \autoref{alb_surj_and_ordinary} (with minor implementations) shows that $a$ is surjective. If we consider the Stein factorization \[ \begin{tikzcd}
			X \arrow[rr, "f"] \arrow[rrrr, "a"', bend right] &  & S \arrow[rr, "g"] &  & A
		\end{tikzcd} \] of $a$, then one can show by some trickery that $V^0_{\injj}(g_*\omega) \inc V^0_{\injj}(a_*\omega_A)$ for some sub--Cartier module $\omega$ of $\omega_S$. Hence, we deduce that $V^0_{\injj}(g_*\omega_S) = \{0\}$ and we can proceed somewhat similarly to the second strategy of the above paragraph.

		\item Relaxing the assumption $\kappa(X, \omega_X) = 0$ to only $h^0(X, \cO_X(2K_X)) = 1$. This is definitely the hardest generalities, and it requires new ideas. The only way the author knows to make this work is to prove a positive characteristic version of the Pareschi's beautiful argument in \cite{Pareschi_Basic_results_on_irr_vars_via_FM_methods}. Although this argument seems to requires a lot of ``characteristic zero only'' tools (e.g. Grauert--Riemenschneider vanishing and Kollár's torsion--freeness and splitting theorems), one can work their way in a rather explicit way and mimic the proof. This is carried in \cite{Baudin_Effective_Characterization_of_ordinary_abelian_varieties}.
	\end{itemize}
\end{rem}


\fi
