\subsection{Metrics}
\label{A.3}

Let \(r_i\) denote the daily log-return. The Sharpe ratio (SHR) \citep{RePEc:ucp:jnlbus:v:39:y:1965:p:119} and Sortino ratio (SOR) \citep{Sortino1994PerformanceMI} are computed as follows:
\[
\text{SHR} = \frac{\bar{r}_i}{\sqrt{\frac{\sum_i (r_i - \bar{r}_i)^2}{n - 1}}},
\quad
\text{SOR} = \frac{\bar{r}_i}{\sqrt{\frac{\sum_i \min\bigl(r_i - \bar{r}_i, 0\bigr)^2}{n - 1}}}.
\]
I do not specify a risk-free rate or a minimum acceptable return (MAR) in this study. Therefore, I use the mean return in place of the MAR in the Sortino ratio’s denominator and calculate semi-variance as an alternative to Downside Deviation. I do not subtract the risk-free rate in the numerators of either the Sharpe or Sortino ratios. Also, Sharpe ratio and Sortino ratio are annualized by multiplying by the square root of the number of days.

